WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:12.320
Hello, everyone, and welcome
to lecture 3 of CME 295.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.480
So today is an
exciting day because we

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:18.200
will learn how to actually
train a large language

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:22.520
model in a way that
aligns with our end goal.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:25.300
But before we do
that, as always,

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.480
we will recap what
we saw last time.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.240
So if you remember,
last time, we

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:35.320
talked about how we could use
the transformer architecture

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:39.240
that we had seen in lecture
1 and, in particular,

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.880
see if there is any
component of the transformer

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.080
that we could leverage
for something bigger.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.680
So we saw that the
transformer was composed

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.800
of an encoder and a decoder.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:57.120
And in particular, if you
remove the encoder part,

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.810
you could have a much
simpler architecture

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:07.050
that would reframe the problem
as a text-to-text problem.

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:12.490
And we saw that if we were
to scale such an architecture

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:16.530
in terms of number of
parameters-- so model size,

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:20.370
number of tokens that
the model is trained on--

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.610
we saw a better and
better performance.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:26.930
So these were the
scaling laws that we saw.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:29.690
But something else
that we saw is

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:33.890
that, given that we only have
a limited amount of compute,

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:38.330
that in order for us, given
some amount of compute,

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:41.490
to use it in the
best way possible,

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:46.770
that we should be careful
to not undertrain our model.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.290
And in particular, there was
a paper, the Chinchilla paper,

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:54.670
that saw that actually,
roughly speaking,

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:59.740
for every parameter you have to
actually train it on 20 tokens.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:02.660
And it found that many of the
architectures that were scaled

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:06.540
up were actually undertrained.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:10.539
And then, given that
having bigger models lead

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:14.780
to better performance,
we then wondered

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:19.900
if actually activating all the
parameters was actually useful.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.540
So we saw a class
of models called

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:25.980
mixture of experts,
which selected

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:31.100
specific experts to activate
for a given forward pass.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:34.340
And we saw that
such an architecture

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:37.380
was something we could place
within the feedforward neural

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:43.860
network, which was where most
of the compute was happening.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.220
And we saw a bunch
of other methods

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:51.460
that improved the way we use
transformer-based architectures

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:52.880
since it was first released.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.280
So in particular, we saw
the position embeddings,

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.660
which was actually
something that

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:01.280
would be better to happen
within the attention layer.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:02.660
So we saw RoPE.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:06.960
We saw some other forms of
attention-- so not global,

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.160
but Sliding Window.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.500
We saw a Group Query
Attention as a way

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:16.060
to save on the keys and values
that we need to store in order

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:18.340
to decode our sequence.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:22.460
And we also saw some
normalizing methods.

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:24.820
And this is where we left off--

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.140
lecture 2.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:29.220
And as I mentioned,
this lecture is

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:33.540
about learning how we can
actually train a large language

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:35.300
model.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:38.700
So we're going to start
with the first step, which

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.820
is called pretraining.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.700
And I want to motivate
that step by telling you

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:49.340
about how people usually
trained models traditionally.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:53.790
So if this class was being
held maybe 15 years ago,

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:59.790
we would actually try to train
models on a per-use-case basis.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:02.010
So if, for instance, we
wanted to detect spam,

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:05.670
we would have one model for spam
detection, another model for,

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:08.950
let's say, sentiment extraction,
another one for, let's say,

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:12.510
translation and so on.

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:19.430
But the idea here is to actually
leverage what we learn in one

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:22.110
go and re-use that
for other use cases.

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:25.790
So this is also something that
is known as transfer learning.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:28.950
So actually, the idea
behind what we want to do

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:32.870
is to do a one-time
big what we call

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:36.950
pretraining to
learn about language

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:40.230
and then start from there
if we want to specialize

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:43.710
that for a specific use case.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.230
So this is the idea.

00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:48.270
We want to have the first
step of our training

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:52.700
process as being something
that learns about language.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:54.580
So it's called pretraining.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.960
And we're going to
talk more about this.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:00.240
And then the second
stage is more about

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:02.560
tuning this for a
specific use case.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.400
So in the previous
example, let's assume

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.060
we don't have a
general-purpose model

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:09.460
but we have one big
pretrained model.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:14.600
We would tune that pretrained
model for this specific task.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:18.360
And we will actually see
that LLMs are general enough

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:21.880
to be able to actually
tackle a bunch of tasks

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:24.100
without us having
to retrain those,

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:28.480
but we will see
that more later on.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:29.180
Cool.

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:33.200
So as I mentioned,
this first step

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.880
is about learning
about language.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:40.980
So what we want to do is
to see a bunch of texts,

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:45.800
a bunch of codes, a bunch
of generated generations,

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:47.720
all over the internet.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:51.890
So here, typically, we would
assemble pieces of text

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:54.490
in English, but also
in other languages.

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:57.950
We would assemble, let's
say, codes in Python,

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:02.530
in other languages-- math,
science, you name it.

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:09.850
And we would train our model in
a next-token-prediction fashion.

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:13.730
So I'm going to tell you
exactly how that would work.

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:18.010
So let's assume we have a
piece of text on which we want

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:20.930
our model to be pretrained on.

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:27.530
So here what we would try to do
is to feed some text as input

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:32.650
and ask the model to
predict the next token,

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:38.330
and repeat that process for
all the tokens in the text.

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:41.130
So in this example, let's assume
we have this piece of text,

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:44.910
and we tokenize that with
your favorite tokenizer.

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:49.180
So here it's an arbitrary
way of tokenizing things.

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:53.840
And what we would do is we would
take our LLM, which, as we saw,

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:58.780
is typically a
decoder-only-based architecture,

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:01.460
and we would feed
in the first token.

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:04.780
And what we would do is try
to predict the next token.

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:09.460
So here, the very beginning
of the prediction task,

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:12.180
we only have the
beginning-of-sequence token

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:13.040
that is fed.

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:16.220
And here we would try to
predict the first token.

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:19.660
And then we would
input that token

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:24.000
to try to predict the next
one, and so on and so forth.

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:32.340
And actually, you don't
need to do that sequentially

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:34.140
when you do your
pretraining, because it's

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:36.340
highly parallelizable.

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:42.460
So what you would actually do is
feed the entire input as inputs

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:47.330
and try to predict the next
token for all these inputs.

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:51.570
So it's highly parallelizable.

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:54.990
You can think of it as a huge
matrix multiplication that

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:56.830
is happening under the hood.

00:07:56.830 --> 00:07:58.670
And it's something
that you will also

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:04.210
find as being mentioned as being
teacher-forcing because here,

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:07.408
no matter what the LLM
predicts-- so by the way,

00:08:07.408 --> 00:08:08.450
it's the same thing here.

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:11.750
No matter what the LLM
predicts, we always

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:15.310
put as input for the
next step which word

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:17.070
it should have predicted.

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:20.190
So we're forcing the
model, given some input,

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:25.710
to predict the next token
that is in that text.

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:29.490
And we saw that this
is possible because--

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:32.909
oh, you have a question.

00:08:32.909 --> 00:08:33.890
So excellent question.

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:36.789
The question is, well,
if you teacher-force

00:08:36.789 --> 00:08:38.330
the model to learn
a certain way,

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:40.770
it is not actually
having things.

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:42.770
It is generating
itself as inputs.

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:45.850
So there is a mismatch between
training and inference.

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:47.290
So that's an excellent point.

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:50.590
And we will see, actually, how
we can solve that later on.

00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:52.170
So it's a great remark.

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:54.310
We will see some methods.

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:56.790
Yeah?

00:08:56.790 --> 00:08:59.830
So the question is, if you give
only the beginning of sequence,

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:02.970
the LLM has no information,
and so do you give a prompt?

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:05.070
So the answer is
not at this stage.

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:08.190
But if you don't give
anything to your LLM,

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:11.110
it will learn to just start the
sequence with something that

00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:12.530
starts the sequence.

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:15.790
So it will learn
tokens that could

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:18.810
be likely to appear at the
beginning of the sequence.

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:21.050
But to your point,
there is no information.

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:25.550
So-- yeah.

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:26.890
Cool.

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:29.950
One thing I wanted
to add here is

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:33.510
that we can do that in a
parallel fashion because

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:36.230
of a trick that I
believe we briefly

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:42.120
saw during lecture 1, which is
the masked self-attention that

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:46.520
allows you to only
attend to tokens that

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:49.760
are before you and yourself.

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:52.880
So in other words, if you
want to predict, let's say,

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:59.320
the next token after "teddy,"
you would only be able to attend

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:04.680
to the token "teddy" and
all the tokens before you,

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:09.440
thanks to the Masked
self-attention.

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:12.920
Great, and so just formalizing
that a little bit more,

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:15.800
so what we're doing is
we're inputting something

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:17.600
into the LLM,
which you can think

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:23.320
of it as a sequence of tokens,
until, let's say, t minus 1.

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:26.360
And what you want
to do is the LLM

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:31.360
to actually produce the
probability of the next token

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:33.720
given your input.

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:36.720
And so in a more
formal fashion, you

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:41.330
will use the cross-entropy
loss in order to do that.

00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:43.590
So I have a formula
on the slide,

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:47.850
but I'm going to just derive
it from first principles.

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:56.170
So as with a usual training
setup, what we're trying to do

00:10:56.170 --> 00:11:00.890
is to learn
parameters theta such

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:05.530
that the probability of our
model seeing the training data

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:08.370
is maximized.

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:12.410
So the probability
of the sequence--

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:21.250
you can think of it as p of
the token-- so x1 until xn.

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:24.050
And this probability
is something

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:26.050
that is produced by the model.

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:30.410
So it's parameterized by theta.

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:35.250
So what you want to do is to
find the parameters theta that

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:41.860
maximize the probability of a
model seeing the training set.

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:46.180
So your goal is to
maximize this quantity.

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:50.900
And just going back to
the basics of probability,

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:54.060
it just turns out
that you can have

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:58.040
some other way of writing this.

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:09.060
So you have p of x1 times p of
x2 given x1 times all the way

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:17.820
to p of x, let's say, n,
given x1 until xn minus 1,

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:22.540
which you can rewrite
with this product.

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:30.160
So it's t from 1 to n of p of
theta of xt given your input.

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:34.480
You want to maximize
this quantity.

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:38.570
The problem with maximizing
the products of probabilities,

00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:45.330
which are each between 0 and 1,
is that the product tends to 0,

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:48.790
so it can lead to
numerical instabilities.

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:51.310
So what people do is
they typically take

00:12:51.310 --> 00:12:53.870
the log of this quantity.

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:56.710
So I'll just do the following.

00:12:56.710 --> 00:13:00.230
We have-- so let's see--

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:01.650
log of this quantity.

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:07.110
So we just take log of this.

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:13.590
And this gets transformed
to a sum of the log

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:14.850
of the probabilities.

00:13:19.070 --> 00:13:22.830
So this is just a
property of the logarithm.

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:28.630
And then what we do is we want
to maximize this quantity.

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:31.150
But in machine learning,
what we like to do

00:13:31.150 --> 00:13:33.310
is to minimize things.

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:39.590
So we actually minimize
the minus of this quantity.

00:13:39.590 --> 00:13:42.510
And then we just want to
not have this quantity be

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:44.990
a function of the number of
tokens in the sequence in terms

00:13:44.990 --> 00:13:49.750
of scale, which is why we
divide by the number of tokens.

00:13:49.750 --> 00:13:52.370
And this is our loss.

00:13:59.990 --> 00:14:01.570
And it's a function of theta.

00:14:01.570 --> 00:14:04.050
And the theta comes
from the p of theta,

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:07.150
which is something
that our model outputs

00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:09.910
in the last layer--

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:13.390
so all that to say that you
will find the cross-entropy loss

00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:15.270
in a lot of places.

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:17.890
And just instead of
memorizing this formula,

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:19.970
just think of it from
first principles.

00:14:19.970 --> 00:14:24.830
It's just you trying to
find parameters theta that

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:31.270
maximize the probability of your
model seeing the training data.

00:14:31.270 --> 00:14:32.180
OK, cool.

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:35.480
So as I mentioned, p theta
is the predicted probability,

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:44.160
exceeds your target token x
less than t is the past tokens.

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:48.200
And big T is the
number of tokens.

00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:53.760
So you will sometimes
see another loss that

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:56.440
is actually the same
loss but rescaled called

00:14:56.440 --> 00:15:01.640
Bits Per Byte, BPB.

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:05.760
And the idea is that in the
cross-entropy loss that I just

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:11.320
mentioned, the absolute
value is actually

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:14.600
a function of your tokenizer.

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:18.480
Depending on how you
tokenize your data,

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:22.240
this value might be different.

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:25.820
So in case you want to
compare your loss between,

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:28.480
let's say, a given model that
has a certain type of tokenizer

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:32.290
and another model that has
another kind of tokenizer,

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:35.850
things may not be
exactly comparable,

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:41.610
which is why sometimes
you will see this loss

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:47.510
expressed in a tokenizer-free
manner where you take that loss,

00:15:47.510 --> 00:15:51.370
you actually multiply this by
the number of tokens divided

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:54.810
by the number of
bytes, and you also

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:58.490
multiply this by
1 over log of 2,

00:15:58.490 --> 00:16:00.690
just so that you can
interpret this as bits,

00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:02.390
which is why bits per byte.

00:16:05.690 --> 00:16:08.690
Cool, OK, so now that we know--

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:11.570
yeah, you have a question.

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:13.430
So the question is, what is B?

00:16:13.430 --> 00:16:17.570
So B is the number of
bytes in your input

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:21.770
because each character
can be encoded with bytes.

00:16:21.770 --> 00:16:22.390
So yeah.

00:16:25.010 --> 00:16:27.090
Yeah?

00:16:27.090 --> 00:16:30.220
So the question is, you use the
masked self-attention so that

00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:32.440
you cannot attend to
something in the future.

00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:35.600
Is it something that is only
for decoder or also for encoder?

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:38.740
So here in this case, we
don't have an encoder,

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:41.100
and we only have a decoder.

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:44.020
And our goal is to
predict the next token.

00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:48.140
We need to make sure that when
we predict the next token,

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:51.820
we can only rely on the things
that we have decoded so far

00:16:51.820 --> 00:16:54.100
to mimic the inference setup.

00:16:54.100 --> 00:16:58.220
So long story short is we're
in a decoder-only setup.

00:16:58.220 --> 00:17:00.440
If we were in an
encoder-only setup,

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:02.760
we could not do this
in this fashion.

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:05.460
We could not predict
tokens one at a time,

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:07.859
unless you do something else.

00:17:07.859 --> 00:17:10.800
And we will see
that in lecture 8.

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:11.300
Yeah.

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:15.940
OK, cool.

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:20.140
So now we know how we can
train our model, which loss we

00:17:20.140 --> 00:17:20.800
can use.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:22.619
So here's the
cross-entropy loss.

00:17:22.619 --> 00:17:25.869
The next question
is, what kind of data

00:17:25.869 --> 00:17:28.190
can we actually
train our model on?

00:17:28.190 --> 00:17:30.110
And so here there
is a lot of effort

00:17:30.110 --> 00:17:32.630
that is actually put
into the kind of data

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:34.990
that you want to train on.

00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:38.050
This data can come from the web.

00:17:38.050 --> 00:17:44.790
It can come from repositories,
from places where there's code.

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:47.350
Lately there's an
emphasis on being

00:17:47.350 --> 00:17:51.310
able to solve things with math,
with science just in general.

00:17:51.310 --> 00:17:56.710
The idea is to have a
big-sized corpus on which you

00:17:56.710 --> 00:18:02.030
can have your model learn about
patterns of language, patterns

00:18:02.030 --> 00:18:06.810
of codes, and it's
typically very big in size.

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:11.710
Think of it as
trillions of tokens.

00:18:11.710 --> 00:18:14.110
And I think last time
there was a question

00:18:14.110 --> 00:18:18.790
on whether we would actually
repeat the same data if we

00:18:18.790 --> 00:18:22.270
train it, or if we only
were seeing unique tokens.

00:18:22.270 --> 00:18:26.470
Well, there has been some papers
actually looking into this.

00:18:26.470 --> 00:18:28.790
And there's one
paper that is here

00:18:28.790 --> 00:18:31.950
on the slide, which
was looking at the case

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:35.030
where you're in a
data-constrained regime

00:18:35.030 --> 00:18:38.790
where you only have, let's
say, a set amount of tokens.

00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:43.990
The question is, would you
rather stop the training,

00:18:43.990 --> 00:18:47.710
or is it worth for you actually
training multiple times

00:18:47.710 --> 00:18:49.390
on the same tokens?

00:18:49.390 --> 00:18:53.850
And the answer in this case is
actually yes, up to four times.

00:18:53.850 --> 00:18:55.830
It's actually something
that is allowing

00:18:55.830 --> 00:18:58.470
you to get better performance.

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:04.150
So there is this trade-off of
whether you would rather see

00:19:04.150 --> 00:19:08.110
more unique tokens-- is it
of high or low quality?--

00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:11.930
and whether you want to
actually repeat that as well.

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:14.170
So that's the question
that people ask themselves.

00:19:17.030 --> 00:19:23.360
Again, on the topic of figuring
out which data to use--

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:26.460
so there's been a lot of work
in trying to figure that out.

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:29.660
And so I'll just cite
this work as well,

00:19:29.660 --> 00:19:36.880
DataComp-LM, which tries to
tease out the impact of having

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:42.040
a high-quality data set versus
having a better architecture,

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:47.600
Because two people can
try to train their model

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:51.320
and wonder if a given data
is of high enough quality

00:19:51.320 --> 00:19:52.585
for their setup.

00:19:52.585 --> 00:19:53.960
But then the
performance could be

00:19:53.960 --> 00:19:58.520
actually due to their
own model architecture.

00:19:58.520 --> 00:20:03.600
So this is trying to normalize
the architecture side so that we

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:09.680
can only tease out the impact of
having a better data versus not.

00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:12.680
There's a lot of work
on that piece as well.

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:14.780
And you can also
wonder, well, OK,

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:18.380
how can I obtain
my final data set?

00:20:18.380 --> 00:20:22.730
Should I put 20% of code,
50% of web, and so on, so on?

00:20:22.730 --> 00:20:26.710
And so here people have been
doing that in multiple ways.

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:32.690
And here one way is to
train multiple small models

00:20:32.690 --> 00:20:36.250
on different mixtures
of data and see

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:41.450
how each of these small models
perform so that we can then

00:20:41.450 --> 00:20:47.450
infer a potential good mix
of data that could work well

00:20:47.450 --> 00:20:50.090
if we were to scale that up.

00:20:50.090 --> 00:20:52.690
So yeah, I highly recommend
also reading that paper in case

00:20:52.690 --> 00:20:54.210
you're interested.

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:58.450
And again, I want to emphasize
that the data, preparation data

00:20:58.450 --> 00:21:01.410
work, is actually
an involved one.

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:07.050
So I wanted to cite this
diagram from Olmol 3, which

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:09.450
is an open-weight model.

00:21:09.450 --> 00:21:13.650
And here it just goes through
the training pipeline, the data

00:21:13.650 --> 00:21:18.180
preparation pipeline,
which is quite involved.

00:21:18.180 --> 00:21:22.900
And as I mentioned, the size
of the pretraining data set

00:21:22.900 --> 00:21:28.580
is typically huge, on the
order of trillions of tokens,

00:21:28.580 --> 00:21:31.460
and lately, even tens
of trillions of tokens.

00:21:31.460 --> 00:21:36.460
So as an example, GLM-5,
which is a relatively recent

00:21:36.460 --> 00:21:41.540
open-weight model, was trained
on 30 trillion tokens, just as

00:21:41.540 --> 00:21:44.780
a point of reference.

00:21:44.780 --> 00:21:50.040
So you can imagine that in
order to train such a big model,

00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:53.380
you need to have the
right infra in place.

00:21:53.380 --> 00:21:55.460
So I just wanted to
say that we will not

00:21:55.460 --> 00:21:58.780
focus on that in this
lecture, but we'll

00:21:58.780 --> 00:22:00.660
focus on that in lecture 5.

00:22:00.660 --> 00:22:06.740
So stay tuned if you're
interested on that topic.

00:22:06.740 --> 00:22:12.260
Cool, so this was
traditionally the first step

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:15.540
that people would do in
order to train their LLMs.

00:22:15.540 --> 00:22:18.630
But in recent years,
there is a second stage

00:22:18.630 --> 00:22:23.350
that has emerged which is
called midtraining, which

00:22:23.350 --> 00:22:28.470
aims at injecting knowledge
on tasks that are more

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:31.550
aligned with your use case.

00:22:31.550 --> 00:22:34.170
So if, for instance, you
care about your model,

00:22:34.170 --> 00:22:37.230
let's say, being able to
reason, what you want to do

00:22:37.230 --> 00:22:41.310
is to expose your model
to more of that code

00:22:41.310 --> 00:22:45.150
and masked text data.

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:48.750
And so here you would
use midtraining and rely

00:22:48.750 --> 00:22:53.470
on higher-quality data, still
doing next-token prediction,

00:22:53.470 --> 00:22:55.830
in order to have
your model learn more

00:22:55.830 --> 00:22:59.830
about these use cases.

00:22:59.830 --> 00:23:01.430
So that's one.

00:23:01.430 --> 00:23:05.550
And the second thing
is training your model

00:23:05.550 --> 00:23:12.870
to see longer contexts, inputs,
because one thing that I did not

00:23:12.870 --> 00:23:16.400
emphasize here is for
pretraining, typically

00:23:16.400 --> 00:23:19.760
for efficiency
purposes, we pretrain

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:23.120
based on sequence
lengths that are

00:23:23.120 --> 00:23:27.240
on the order of several
thousands of tokens.

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:32.320
But in reality, you could
have tens of thousands

00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:35.800
of tokens as inputs or even
hundreds of thousands of tokens.

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:37.880
And you need to teach
your model to actually

00:23:37.880 --> 00:23:40.920
be able to handle that.

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:44.360
So we're going to spend
maybe one or two minutes

00:23:44.360 --> 00:23:46.160
on the long context training.

00:23:46.160 --> 00:23:53.080
So we saw that this was
dependent on our ability

00:23:53.080 --> 00:23:56.000
to encode positions,
which is something

00:23:56.000 --> 00:23:58.000
that nowadays people do.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:00.920
People use RoPE.

00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:04.540
We saw that there is no
learned parameter within RoPE.

00:24:04.540 --> 00:24:09.800
So we could, in theory,
use that out of the box.

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:12.540
But the problem is, if
you don't do anything,

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:16.360
if you just train your
model on short sequences

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:19.920
and want to actually use
that for inference for longer

00:24:19.920 --> 00:24:24.000
sequences, the reality is that
your model actually has never

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:28.380
seen the rotations of
these longer sequences,

00:24:28.380 --> 00:24:31.240
so it may get confused.

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:35.680
So this is the reason why we
have this long context training

00:24:35.680 --> 00:24:41.560
stage where we're trying to
expose our model on these longer

00:24:41.560 --> 00:24:43.440
sequences.

00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:46.840
And in particular, there are
typically some little tweaks

00:24:46.840 --> 00:24:51.080
that are made to
the RoPE frequencies

00:24:51.080 --> 00:24:55.400
in order to help our
model learn better.

00:24:55.400 --> 00:24:58.720
So in particular, you will see
a bunch of extension methods

00:24:58.720 --> 00:24:59.580
from RoPE.

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:00.920
So I'll cite one.

00:25:00.920 --> 00:25:03.080
You may see YaRN.

00:25:03.080 --> 00:25:07.640
And you may wonder, well,
why do you need that?

00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:12.570
So if you were to simply train
your model that you previously

00:25:12.570 --> 00:25:15.050
trained on shorter
contexts, if you

00:25:15.050 --> 00:25:20.610
were to train that model
on long context inputs,

00:25:20.610 --> 00:25:25.410
then you would actually
give your model a hard time

00:25:25.410 --> 00:25:29.930
to learn these longer
context position

00:25:29.930 --> 00:25:31.410
differences, because
this would be

00:25:31.410 --> 00:25:35.730
completely unseen from
the model perspective.

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:39.970
So what people do
is they actually

00:25:39.970 --> 00:25:44.050
interpolate the
frequencies in order

00:25:44.050 --> 00:25:49.450
to have long position
differences be

00:25:49.450 --> 00:25:51.530
within the range of
frequencies that were

00:25:51.530 --> 00:25:55.930
seen during the short
context training.

00:25:55.930 --> 00:25:59.170
So there are some little
tweaks on the frequencies

00:25:59.170 --> 00:26:04.650
that are used by RoPE in order
to help the model learn better.

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:08.100
And in addition to that, there
is this concept of curriculum

00:26:08.100 --> 00:26:12.460
learning, so learning-- yes?

00:26:12.460 --> 00:26:15.420
So the question is,
how do they figure out

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:18.020
what is the length that
they will encounter?

00:26:18.020 --> 00:26:20.500
So it's preplanned.

00:26:20.500 --> 00:26:22.460
So they will say, OK--

00:26:22.460 --> 00:26:26.540
on the figure, actually, you
can see an example from GLM-5.

00:26:26.540 --> 00:26:31.820
The short context training
is done on 4,000 long inputs.

00:26:31.820 --> 00:26:34.140
And then for the longer
context training,

00:26:34.140 --> 00:26:38.540
it's elongated to
128k or even 200k.

00:26:38.540 --> 00:26:41.020
So they know that
number from the start,

00:26:41.020 --> 00:26:43.140
and they will
interpolate accordingly.

00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:47.380
And then they will use
that tweaked frequency

00:26:47.380 --> 00:26:50.540
and do the training and then
let the model learn the weights

00:26:50.540 --> 00:26:52.740
accordingly.

00:26:52.740 --> 00:26:54.920
So the question is,
how about inference?

00:26:54.920 --> 00:26:57.100
So they would use the
tweaked frequencies

00:26:57.100 --> 00:27:00.620
after the long context training.

00:27:00.620 --> 00:27:05.460
So they would initially train
the model for short context

00:27:05.460 --> 00:27:09.930
on some frequencies, and then
they perform midtraining.

00:27:09.930 --> 00:27:11.710
They perform long
context training

00:27:11.710 --> 00:27:14.870
in which they tweak a
little bit the frequencies.

00:27:14.870 --> 00:27:17.830
And then they use these
frequencies for inference

00:27:17.830 --> 00:27:20.150
and so on.

00:27:20.150 --> 00:27:21.750
Yeah?

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:23.830
So the question is,
if you use YaRN,

00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:25.530
do you completely
change your setup?

00:27:25.530 --> 00:27:28.670
So here you actually
rely on the RoPE

00:27:28.670 --> 00:27:34.470
that you had, but you change the
frequencies so that the model is

00:27:34.470 --> 00:27:37.950
more familiar with the ranges
of frequencies encountered

00:27:37.950 --> 00:27:43.210
during long contexts and is
more familiar with that, as in,

00:27:43.210 --> 00:27:46.110
it has seen such frequencies
during the short context

00:27:46.110 --> 00:27:46.690
training.

00:27:50.670 --> 00:27:54.810
Exactly, yeah.

00:27:54.810 --> 00:27:55.490
Great.

00:27:58.230 --> 00:28:00.830
So why is this hard?

00:28:00.830 --> 00:28:05.020
So if you remember, transformer
is based on self-attention.

00:28:05.020 --> 00:28:08.120
Self-attention is everything
attends to everything else--

00:28:08.120 --> 00:28:10.600
O of n squared complexity.

00:28:10.600 --> 00:28:16.360
So if you scale up from 4,000 as
inputs to hundreds of thousands,

00:28:16.360 --> 00:28:19.013
well, you have a lot
more computation.

00:28:19.013 --> 00:28:20.180
But it's not the only thing.

00:28:20.180 --> 00:28:21.960
The second thing
is it's actually

00:28:21.960 --> 00:28:28.360
hard to get data on which
some parts of the data

00:28:28.360 --> 00:28:33.040
need some other parts
in a long range fashion

00:28:33.040 --> 00:28:35.380
in order to predict
that specific token.

00:28:35.380 --> 00:28:37.880
So I'll give you an example.

00:28:37.880 --> 00:28:45.460
You need your model to learn to
use information far in the past

00:28:45.460 --> 00:28:47.460
in order to make a
prediction far in the future.

00:28:47.460 --> 00:28:51.520
So you need to have
some observation

00:28:51.520 --> 00:28:54.540
that makes use of something
that is far in the past

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:57.240
and that is not something
that is trivially--

00:28:57.240 --> 00:28:58.740
something that you
can come up with.

00:28:58.740 --> 00:29:02.440
So there is some amount of
work that is around coming up

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:05.080
with such observations.

00:29:05.080 --> 00:29:07.840
So there's a number
of challenges.

00:29:07.840 --> 00:29:10.320
But at the end of
the day, if you

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:14.240
do your pretraining,
your midtraining,

00:29:14.240 --> 00:29:19.760
and your long context training,
at the end of that stage,

00:29:19.760 --> 00:29:24.720
you will, first of all, have
spent a bunch of dollars.

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:28.600
But then the nice
thing is you will

00:29:28.600 --> 00:29:33.640
have a model that knows about
text, that knows about codes,

00:29:33.640 --> 00:29:36.480
and that has acquired a certain
amount of knowledge that

00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:39.160
was in that data.

00:29:39.160 --> 00:29:41.600
And in particular
here, if you look

00:29:41.600 --> 00:29:44.720
at the model cards of
your favorite models

00:29:44.720 --> 00:29:47.520
on third-party
providers, you will

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:50.960
see that there is a
knowledge cutoff date, which

00:29:50.960 --> 00:29:54.720
is the date that is
used up until which

00:29:54.720 --> 00:29:58.320
you have your training data.

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:01.070
So for instance, in the
case of GPT-6 Astra,

00:30:01.070 --> 00:30:04.450
you know that the data that was
used to train that model was up

00:30:04.450 --> 00:30:09.850
till April 30th of 2026.

00:30:09.850 --> 00:30:15.370
And the problem is that that
knowledge is fixed in time.

00:30:15.370 --> 00:30:17.430
So that means that
out of the box,

00:30:17.430 --> 00:30:20.610
if you were to ask your model
about something that happened

00:30:20.610 --> 00:30:23.290
after that cutoff
date, your model

00:30:23.290 --> 00:30:26.650
would have no idea about
it because it didn't see it

00:30:26.650 --> 00:30:28.630
at training.

00:30:28.630 --> 00:30:31.610
So it doesn't have
the knowledge.

00:30:31.610 --> 00:30:35.390
But we will see techniques
to actually circumvent that.

00:30:35.390 --> 00:30:39.210
So more on that later.

00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:41.010
So I have a question for you.

00:30:41.010 --> 00:30:42.770
We've done all this work.

00:30:42.770 --> 00:30:45.810
We've started from
our LLM, just did

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:48.170
all of that pretraining,
midtraining, and long context

00:30:48.170 --> 00:30:48.670
training.

00:30:48.670 --> 00:30:53.330
And let's suppose we input
the following prompt.

00:30:53.330 --> 00:30:57.970
"Can I put my teddy
bear in the washer?"

00:30:57.970 --> 00:31:02.920
What is one response that the
LLM can give us at that stage?

00:31:07.460 --> 00:31:09.258
Yeah?

00:31:09.258 --> 00:31:10.800
I think you have
the right intuition.

00:31:10.800 --> 00:31:13.280
So here what it
will do is-- well,

00:31:13.280 --> 00:31:14.840
it will not respond
to the question.

00:31:14.840 --> 00:31:18.500
Typically, it will actually
try to autocomplete

00:31:18.500 --> 00:31:20.540
because the model
so far has been

00:31:20.540 --> 00:31:23.560
trained in a next token
prediction fashion.

00:31:23.560 --> 00:31:28.020
It has been trained to
guess the next word.

00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:30.480
So here it will respond
something along these lines.

00:31:30.480 --> 00:31:32.320
So, "Can I put my teddy
bear in the washer?"

00:31:32.320 --> 00:31:35.380
Well, "Teddy bears are often
made of materials like polyester

00:31:35.380 --> 00:31:37.558
and cotton, with plastic
eyes and sometimes small

00:31:37.558 --> 00:31:40.100
accessories"-- typically the
kind of thing that you would see

00:31:40.100 --> 00:31:42.700
in text.

00:31:42.700 --> 00:31:44.700
Is it useful?

00:31:44.700 --> 00:31:46.180
No, it's not useful.

00:31:46.180 --> 00:31:47.980
So we're not done.

00:31:47.980 --> 00:31:51.020
This was pretraining.

00:31:51.020 --> 00:31:53.040
Now let's go into
the next stage,

00:31:53.040 --> 00:31:57.530
which is trying to
make our LLM useful.

00:31:57.530 --> 00:32:01.550
So if we ask it questions,
we want answers.

00:32:01.550 --> 00:32:04.030
So in this stage--

00:32:04.030 --> 00:32:08.350
so first, this stage is called
SFT, Supervised Fine-Tuning.

00:32:08.350 --> 00:32:13.670
And the goal is to change
the model behavior to align

00:32:13.670 --> 00:32:16.190
with what we wanted to do.

00:32:16.190 --> 00:32:19.370
So in the previous example,
if you want to ask a question,

00:32:19.370 --> 00:32:24.990
you want an answer, so you want
to an assistantlike behavior.

00:32:24.990 --> 00:32:28.270
So the idea behind this
stage is to come up

00:32:28.270 --> 00:32:33.230
with input/output pairs for
which you want your model

00:32:33.230 --> 00:32:36.770
to respond like the
output for a given input.

00:32:39.350 --> 00:32:43.310
So here the objective
function will still

00:32:43.310 --> 00:32:45.530
be next token prediction.

00:32:45.530 --> 00:32:48.150
You want some
input, and you want

00:32:48.150 --> 00:32:51.350
to predict what
the next token is.

00:32:51.350 --> 00:32:58.160
So in particular, when it comes
to acquiring a behavior that

00:32:58.160 --> 00:33:01.280
is like an assistant,
you want pairs

00:33:01.280 --> 00:33:04.880
of instructions and responses.

00:33:04.880 --> 00:33:08.640
So as an example, let's
say the instruction can be,

00:33:08.640 --> 00:33:11.280
"Write a short story about a
teddy bear who likes to read

00:33:11.280 --> 00:33:11.780
poetry."

00:33:11.780 --> 00:33:14.960
And then you have
some poem, let's say.

00:33:14.960 --> 00:33:18.020
Another one about "explain why
a teddy bear is a great friend,"

00:33:18.020 --> 00:33:19.840
and then you have the response.

00:33:19.840 --> 00:33:26.600
And what you would do here
is pass the input, which

00:33:26.600 --> 00:33:30.200
is the instruction,
into your LLM,

00:33:30.200 --> 00:33:32.920
and try to predict
the next token based

00:33:32.920 --> 00:33:38.560
on that input, via the
next-token-prediction objective

00:33:38.560 --> 00:33:41.760
function, which here we
saw is, for instance, done

00:33:41.760 --> 00:33:45.040
via the cross-entropy loss.

00:33:45.040 --> 00:33:47.540
So to make sure we're all
on the same page here,

00:33:47.540 --> 00:33:49.140
the instruction
could be in blue.

00:33:49.140 --> 00:33:51.200
And then what we
would try to do is

00:33:51.200 --> 00:33:55.170
to have our model
predict the ones

00:33:55.170 --> 00:33:58.850
in pink, which is the answer.

00:33:58.850 --> 00:34:01.610
So the setup is slightly
different with respect

00:34:01.610 --> 00:34:02.470
to pretraining.

00:34:02.470 --> 00:34:04.810
Yes?

00:34:04.810 --> 00:34:08.350
So the question is, how is SFT
different from midtraining?

00:34:08.350 --> 00:34:11.810
So midtraining is about
higher-quality data.

00:34:11.810 --> 00:34:14.530
But you don't have one
specific prompt as input

00:34:14.530 --> 00:34:18.210
that you're trying to just
mimic a behavior as output.

00:34:18.210 --> 00:34:20.889
It is still a corpus of
texts for which you're trying

00:34:20.889 --> 00:34:23.929
to predict the next token.

00:34:23.929 --> 00:34:26.250
The main difference is
in midtraining compared

00:34:26.250 --> 00:34:30.650
to pretraining, you have
higher-quality data and data

00:34:30.650 --> 00:34:33.462
that is more aligned with
the task of interest.

00:34:33.462 --> 00:34:35.670
So if you're interested in,
let's say, math problems,

00:34:35.670 --> 00:34:38.730
you would have more
math-related data.

00:34:38.730 --> 00:34:41.730
But here the idea
is specifically

00:34:41.730 --> 00:34:45.050
to tweak your behavior,
the behavior of a model,

00:34:45.050 --> 00:34:47.090
to align with what you want.

00:34:47.090 --> 00:34:52.090
And in this example, we're
passing it an input x.

00:34:52.090 --> 00:34:56.929
So we're not telling our model
to predict the x's as input.

00:34:56.929 --> 00:34:59.810
And what we want it
to do is to predict

00:34:59.810 --> 00:35:03.410
the next token of the answer.

00:35:03.410 --> 00:35:06.730
So the slight change with
respect to the previous setup

00:35:06.730 --> 00:35:14.570
is the input here is x, the
instruction or the prompts,

00:35:14.570 --> 00:35:18.770
and then whatever answer
that has been decoded so far.

00:35:18.770 --> 00:35:23.250
And what you want to do is to
predict the next token, again,

00:35:23.250 --> 00:35:27.250
with a cross-entropy-like loss.

00:35:27.250 --> 00:35:32.370
So you would have the log of
the probability of the target

00:35:32.370 --> 00:35:36.090
token given the input prompt
and the past tokens, typically

00:35:36.090 --> 00:35:39.050
averaged across tokens.

00:35:39.050 --> 00:35:41.490
So in terms of the kind of
data that you would use,

00:35:41.490 --> 00:35:44.390
you would use data that is
aligned with what you want.

00:35:44.390 --> 00:35:49.020
So here I mentioned one kind
of data, which is assistance-

00:35:49.020 --> 00:35:50.920
or instruction-related data.

00:35:50.920 --> 00:35:53.900
But you could very well
have other kinds of data.

00:35:53.900 --> 00:35:55.620
So let's assume you
want, I don't know,

00:35:55.620 --> 00:35:59.520
specifically writing about
poems in a very specific style.

00:35:59.520 --> 00:36:02.860
You would have only
data about that.

00:36:02.860 --> 00:36:09.020
And the size of your SFT data,
which would be much lower

00:36:09.020 --> 00:36:11.420
compared to the
pretraining one--

00:36:11.420 --> 00:36:14.880
so if, for instance, it is
a very specific use case,

00:36:14.880 --> 00:36:18.700
you would have of
hundreds or thousands--

00:36:18.700 --> 00:36:21.540
hundreds or thousands.

00:36:21.540 --> 00:36:23.500
But if you want something
bigger, let's say,

00:36:23.500 --> 00:36:27.520
having an assistant-like
behavior on a variety of tasks,

00:36:27.520 --> 00:36:30.060
you would have maybe
millions of examples,

00:36:30.060 --> 00:36:32.140
but much lower
magnitude compared

00:36:32.140 --> 00:36:33.680
to the pretraining stage.

00:36:36.300 --> 00:36:42.180
So back to our example-- let's
assume I am asking my LLM,

00:36:42.180 --> 00:36:45.740
"Can I put my teddy
bear in the washer?"

00:36:45.740 --> 00:36:49.470
What is the kind of-- yes?

00:36:49.470 --> 00:36:52.970
So the question is, do you
penalize predicting the input?

00:36:52.970 --> 00:36:54.110
The answer is no.

00:36:54.110 --> 00:36:57.358
You only penalize
predicting the output.

00:36:57.358 --> 00:36:58.650
You don't care about the input.

00:36:58.650 --> 00:36:59.850
The input is given to you.

00:37:04.230 --> 00:37:06.710
So from this formula
you would have,

00:37:06.710 --> 00:37:09.670
the sum over t of
the p theta of--

00:37:09.670 --> 00:37:13.230
log of p theta of
t given x and y.

00:37:13.230 --> 00:37:16.810
Yeah, x is an input, yes, yeah.

00:37:20.870 --> 00:37:23.840
So the question is, if you
compute the loss of the prompt,

00:37:23.840 --> 00:37:25.090
would it hurt the performance?

00:37:25.090 --> 00:37:26.450
So you would not really.

00:37:26.450 --> 00:37:27.930
So it's not your intention.

00:37:27.930 --> 00:37:31.030
Your intention is to not
predict what the prompt is.

00:37:31.030 --> 00:37:33.750
So it's just not aligned
with what you want to do.

00:37:33.750 --> 00:37:35.950
Yeah.

00:37:35.950 --> 00:37:39.310
Any other questions?

00:37:39.310 --> 00:37:44.590
OK, back to the question
that I had for you-- so

00:37:44.590 --> 00:37:48.140
let's assume we have the
same inputs as last time,

00:37:48.140 --> 00:37:52.440
but this time with an LLM that
is not only pretrained, but also

00:37:52.440 --> 00:37:55.720
instruction-tuned with SFT.

00:37:55.720 --> 00:37:59.360
What would be the kind of
response you would get?

00:37:59.360 --> 00:38:01.880
No.

00:38:01.880 --> 00:38:05.800
Yeah, exactly, no, but it will
say also some other things.

00:38:05.800 --> 00:38:07.940
Maybe, "No, it
might get damaged,

00:38:07.940 --> 00:38:12.880
try hand-washing instead,"
which is much better because it

00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:16.440
answers your question.

00:38:16.440 --> 00:38:21.120
But maybe you're
someone who wants

00:38:21.120 --> 00:38:26.360
to have an answer that is
a bit more friendly, maybe

00:38:26.360 --> 00:38:29.920
with some specific tone.

00:38:29.920 --> 00:38:35.560
So the next section will
be about trying to see

00:38:35.560 --> 00:38:38.880
how we can improve that answer.

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:42.680
But before we do that,
I wanted to just go over

00:38:42.680 --> 00:38:47.050
some challenges
of the SFT stage.

00:38:47.050 --> 00:38:49.070
So the first one
is, as I mentioned,

00:38:49.070 --> 00:38:51.370
we need very high-quality
data because when

00:38:51.370 --> 00:38:54.370
we train our model
on that SFT data,

00:38:54.370 --> 00:38:58.070
the model will really behave
exactly like we told it to.

00:38:58.070 --> 00:39:00.690
So we really need to make
sure that the data is

00:39:00.690 --> 00:39:04.050
exactly what we want.

00:39:04.050 --> 00:39:07.450
The other thing is, if we have
too much data with respect

00:39:07.450 --> 00:39:09.890
to one specific
piece, the model may

00:39:09.890 --> 00:39:12.342
be biased to answer in
a very specific way.

00:39:12.342 --> 00:39:14.050
So there are some
challenges with respect

00:39:14.050 --> 00:39:18.290
to just making sure we
have the right distribution

00:39:18.290 --> 00:39:20.930
of the prompts that
we're tuning it on,

00:39:20.930 --> 00:39:23.050
along with being
able to generalize

00:39:23.050 --> 00:39:24.850
and actually evaluating it.

00:39:24.850 --> 00:39:28.710
There's one thing that I want
to spend a few minutes on,

00:39:28.710 --> 00:39:32.730
which is the computational part.

00:39:32.730 --> 00:39:36.690
So as we mentioned,
our LLM is typically

00:39:36.690 --> 00:39:38.930
on the order of
billions of parameters

00:39:38.930 --> 00:39:43.770
or even tens or hundreds
of billions of parameters.

00:39:43.770 --> 00:39:49.190
And your model knows
already a lot about text,

00:39:49.190 --> 00:39:51.250
already knows a lot.

00:39:51.250 --> 00:39:53.930
And what you're
doing here is trying

00:39:53.930 --> 00:39:58.810
to just steer its behavior
towards something that you

00:39:58.810 --> 00:40:02.850
would like the model to behave.

00:40:02.850 --> 00:40:05.250
So the question
is, do you really

00:40:05.250 --> 00:40:09.810
need to tweak all
the parameters,

00:40:09.810 --> 00:40:14.410
or can you actually get away
by just tweaking some of them?

00:40:14.410 --> 00:40:18.210
And this is exactly
what people have asked.

00:40:18.210 --> 00:40:20.870
And this is something
that motivates LoRA.

00:40:20.870 --> 00:40:23.170
So who has heard of LoRA?

00:40:23.170 --> 00:40:23.708
Yeah?

00:40:23.708 --> 00:40:24.250
Other people?

00:40:24.250 --> 00:40:24.870
Great.

00:40:24.870 --> 00:40:28.410
So LoRA stands for
Low-Rank Adaptation.

00:40:28.410 --> 00:40:31.850
And the idea behind
LoRA is you actually

00:40:31.850 --> 00:40:35.010
probably don't need to update
all the weights of your model

00:40:35.010 --> 00:40:36.770
in order to behave
a certain way,

00:40:36.770 --> 00:40:39.900
because you're
almost there already.

00:40:39.900 --> 00:40:46.500
So the idea is you take the
learnable parameters that you

00:40:46.500 --> 00:40:51.540
have, which is
typically as a matrix,

00:40:51.540 --> 00:40:55.420
and you decompose that
as your pretrained,

00:40:55.420 --> 00:41:04.180
already-learned weights, W0, and
then some tunable parameters.

00:41:04.180 --> 00:41:07.060
And these tunable
parameters, they're

00:41:07.060 --> 00:41:12.660
actually living in much
lower-rank matrices,

00:41:12.660 --> 00:41:18.075
so here B and A. And
it slashes completely

00:41:18.075 --> 00:41:20.200
the number of parameters
that you need to train on.

00:41:20.200 --> 00:41:25.020
So the good thing is
it's much more efficient,

00:41:25.020 --> 00:41:27.260
and it turns out to
be actually leading

00:41:27.260 --> 00:41:30.180
to roughly similar
performance, depending

00:41:30.180 --> 00:41:35.140
on the size of your data and
your task, which is great.

00:41:35.140 --> 00:41:39.890
And also-- let's assume,
back to our initial example,

00:41:39.890 --> 00:41:42.990
you have several use
cases, and you, let's say,

00:41:42.990 --> 00:41:47.710
want to tune your model with
LoRA for each specific use case.

00:41:47.710 --> 00:41:50.990
The good thing is
that you only need

00:41:50.990 --> 00:41:55.830
to store the pretrained
weights, W0, and then

00:41:55.830 --> 00:42:00.010
the smaller matrices that
are task-specific matrices.

00:42:00.010 --> 00:42:03.590
So here, for spam,
if you train LoRA,

00:42:03.590 --> 00:42:10.110
you get B and A that are
specific to the spam use case.

00:42:10.110 --> 00:42:12.630
For sentiment, you
can reuse W0, but then

00:42:12.630 --> 00:42:15.550
have the B and A tuned
specifically for the sentiment

00:42:15.550 --> 00:42:19.990
extraction task-- same
for translation and so on.

00:42:19.990 --> 00:42:24.750
So in other words, if
you didn't use LoRA,

00:42:24.750 --> 00:42:28.510
you would have to
store all the weights

00:42:28.510 --> 00:42:30.850
for the first one, all the
weights for the second one,

00:42:30.850 --> 00:42:31.410
and so on.

00:42:31.410 --> 00:42:37.520
But here you only need
to store the delta.

00:42:37.520 --> 00:42:41.720
So it's also great
for storage purposes.

00:42:41.720 --> 00:42:47.520
So originally, the
authors of LoRA, they

00:42:47.520 --> 00:42:54.160
mentioned people should do
that on the matrices that

00:42:54.160 --> 00:42:57.360
are within the
self-attention layer.

00:42:57.360 --> 00:43:00.280
But since then, people have
tried different setups,

00:43:00.280 --> 00:43:04.920
and they have experimented
with different data sets.

00:43:04.920 --> 00:43:09.480
And the latest recommendation
is to actually also leverage

00:43:09.480 --> 00:43:13.080
the matrices that are within
the feedforward neural network,

00:43:13.080 --> 00:43:15.620
because it's actually where
all the compute is happening.

00:43:15.620 --> 00:43:18.560
This is where a lot
of the parameters are.

00:43:18.560 --> 00:43:22.100
So it actually leads to a
lot of performance gain.

00:43:22.100 --> 00:43:27.160
And as a suggested reading,
there is a great article,

00:43:27.160 --> 00:43:28.880
I believe, from
Thinking Machine that

00:43:28.880 --> 00:43:31.660
is worth reading that is at
the bottom of the slides.

00:43:31.660 --> 00:43:36.370
So I highly recommend taking
a look if you're interested.

00:43:36.370 --> 00:43:38.450
Cool.

00:43:38.450 --> 00:43:40.450
Yeah?

00:43:40.450 --> 00:43:43.190
So the question is, let's assume
I'm asking a given question.

00:43:43.190 --> 00:43:47.290
How do I know which
model takes the task?

00:43:47.290 --> 00:43:50.450
Well, the answer here is
you would typically not

00:43:50.450 --> 00:43:52.250
have different
weights if you want

00:43:52.250 --> 00:43:54.530
a general-purpose assistant.

00:43:54.530 --> 00:43:56.570
And we'll see this
a little bit later.

00:43:56.570 --> 00:43:59.170
Typically, you would
train one big model

00:43:59.170 --> 00:44:01.290
and have it have as
much knowledge that you

00:44:01.290 --> 00:44:06.730
want, as much knowledge as you
can, and answer these questions.

00:44:06.730 --> 00:44:11.410
So here it's mostly if you have
some very, very specific use

00:44:11.410 --> 00:44:12.450
cases.

00:44:12.450 --> 00:44:14.930
Let's suppose something
extremely custom, and you need,

00:44:14.930 --> 00:44:17.730
let's say, fine-tuning for that.

00:44:17.730 --> 00:44:23.130
But for the general case, you
typically would not do that.

00:44:23.130 --> 00:44:24.930
Yeah?

00:44:24.930 --> 00:44:30.450
So the question is, you said the
data that is required for SFT

00:44:30.450 --> 00:44:33.770
is 10 to the power of 6, which
is several order of magnitude

00:44:33.770 --> 00:44:36.070
less than for pretraining.

00:44:36.070 --> 00:44:37.990
So it's less than pretraining.

00:44:37.990 --> 00:44:38.990
Why don't you do that?

00:44:38.990 --> 00:44:41.410
Well, people who
want to fine-tune,

00:44:41.410 --> 00:44:43.930
they're usually people
like you and I. They

00:44:43.930 --> 00:44:46.610
don't have super big hardware.

00:44:46.610 --> 00:44:50.090
And so we cannot afford having
to tune billions and hundreds

00:44:50.090 --> 00:44:53.490
of billions of parameters.

00:44:53.490 --> 00:44:54.270
So that's one.

00:44:54.270 --> 00:44:59.330
The second thing is here,
your model is already there.

00:44:59.330 --> 00:45:00.910
It has all the knowledge.

00:45:00.910 --> 00:45:03.170
It has everything.

00:45:03.170 --> 00:45:07.370
It just is not as efficient
to have to retune everything,

00:45:07.370 --> 00:45:09.250
considering that
you're almost there.

00:45:09.250 --> 00:45:12.250
So it's also, just when
you think about it,

00:45:12.250 --> 00:45:14.850
you don't need all
these degrees of freedom

00:45:14.850 --> 00:45:17.850
to get where you want to be.

00:45:17.850 --> 00:45:20.650
So the question
is, are the models

00:45:20.650 --> 00:45:23.310
across different companies
different after this stage?

00:45:23.310 --> 00:45:27.430
So the answer is, it depends on
the architecture that you chose.

00:45:27.430 --> 00:45:29.300
It depends on the
kind of data mixture

00:45:29.300 --> 00:45:30.780
that they're training this on.

00:45:30.780 --> 00:45:34.520
And it varies quite a
lot from model to model.

00:45:34.520 --> 00:45:37.300
So yeah, they can be
different in some way that

00:45:37.300 --> 00:45:40.500
is determined by these
two factors, typically.

00:45:40.500 --> 00:45:42.480
So yeah, but the
data matters a lot.

00:45:45.940 --> 00:45:46.540
Cool.

00:45:49.340 --> 00:45:55.540
Now I want to just motivate
the need-- oh, yes?

00:45:55.540 --> 00:45:57.512
So the question is,
how often do you update

00:45:57.512 --> 00:45:58.720
the parameters of your model?

00:45:58.720 --> 00:46:00.920
Well it depends which
use case you have.

00:46:00.920 --> 00:46:03.980
So I would say if
you're in a use case

00:46:03.980 --> 00:46:08.220
where you want your model
to do a very specific task

00:46:08.220 --> 00:46:11.500
and you care, let's
say, about costs,

00:46:11.500 --> 00:46:14.580
you don't want to
have a big prompt that

00:46:14.580 --> 00:46:17.620
always tells your model to
behave in a certain way.

00:46:17.620 --> 00:46:21.460
Maybe you want to tweak
the weights of your model

00:46:21.460 --> 00:46:23.720
once with this
fine-tuning process,

00:46:23.720 --> 00:46:27.750
and then re-use that
model many times.

00:46:27.750 --> 00:46:30.730
That is one example, but it
highly depends on your use case,

00:46:30.730 --> 00:46:31.250
I would say.

00:46:33.910 --> 00:46:35.270
Cool.

00:46:35.270 --> 00:46:40.190
OK, so far, we've
seen how to have

00:46:40.190 --> 00:46:44.090
our model learn more about
knowledge, about text,

00:46:44.090 --> 00:46:45.310
about code.

00:46:45.310 --> 00:46:48.670
We learned about how to steer
the behavior of our model

00:46:48.670 --> 00:46:51.250
towards something that
we want-- for instance,

00:46:51.250 --> 00:46:52.910
answering questions.

00:46:52.910 --> 00:46:56.830
In this section, we will
see how we can actually

00:46:56.830 --> 00:47:02.430
have our model align with
human preference data.

00:47:02.430 --> 00:47:06.630
And in particular, I want
to motivate this section

00:47:06.630 --> 00:47:08.990
with the following example.

00:47:08.990 --> 00:47:12.190
So let's assume we
have our LLM, which we

00:47:12.190 --> 00:47:14.750
have pretrained and fine-tuned.

00:47:14.750 --> 00:47:20.257
So it knows how to respond to
questions or to instructions.

00:47:20.257 --> 00:47:22.590
So let's suppose we have
"Suggest a new activity I could

00:47:22.590 --> 00:47:24.110
do with my teddy bear."

00:47:24.110 --> 00:47:27.600
And our LLM says, well, "I'd
suggest you actually do not

00:47:27.600 --> 00:47:29.840
spend time with the teddy bear."

00:47:29.840 --> 00:47:32.440
Well, it's an answer, but
it's maybe not the answer

00:47:32.440 --> 00:47:34.960
that you wanted to output.

00:47:34.960 --> 00:47:39.040
What you want is
to tell your model

00:47:39.040 --> 00:47:43.080
that this is not the kind
of output that you want.

00:47:43.080 --> 00:47:48.320
So, so far, we've only
told our model what to do.

00:47:48.320 --> 00:47:53.093
We have not told
it what to not do,

00:47:53.093 --> 00:47:54.760
because, so far, we've
been only saying,

00:47:54.760 --> 00:47:56.300
OK, next token prediction.

00:47:56.300 --> 00:47:58.160
Given this input, I want this.

00:47:58.160 --> 00:48:00.220
And to your point, you
had a great point earlier,

00:48:00.220 --> 00:48:03.920
which is that this
data is not something

00:48:03.920 --> 00:48:06.582
that the model has produced.

00:48:06.582 --> 00:48:07.540
It is actually modeled.

00:48:07.540 --> 00:48:10.760
We are forcing that data in.

00:48:10.760 --> 00:48:14.240
And so this is what we
want to resolve here.

00:48:14.240 --> 00:48:16.400
So in order to resolve
that, what we do,

00:48:16.400 --> 00:48:21.600
the first stage is to collect
preference-paired data.

00:48:21.600 --> 00:48:24.210
So here for a given
prompts, we would

00:48:24.210 --> 00:48:28.330
collect the negative piece,
which is here, not spending time

00:48:28.330 --> 00:48:31.610
with your teddy bear, and then a
positive example, which is maybe

00:48:31.610 --> 00:48:35.170
something that you
want about them

00:48:35.170 --> 00:48:37.590
being great companions
for sleep and so on.

00:48:37.590 --> 00:48:43.490
So you would have prompts with
positive/negative example.

00:48:43.490 --> 00:48:48.810
And the reason why you would
frame that as a pairwise problem

00:48:48.810 --> 00:48:53.390
is just because it is
easier to collect such data.

00:48:53.390 --> 00:48:57.090
So as a human, it is
much simpler for you

00:48:57.090 --> 00:49:00.830
to figure out that something
is better than something else,

00:49:00.830 --> 00:49:03.170
as opposed to stating
that something

00:49:03.170 --> 00:49:05.030
is good in absolute scale.

00:49:07.690 --> 00:49:11.330
Also, for SFT, I mentioned that
the distribution of prompts

00:49:11.330 --> 00:49:14.570
is typically something
that causes the model

00:49:14.570 --> 00:49:15.710
to be quite sensitive.

00:49:15.710 --> 00:49:18.170
So here the great
thing we will see

00:49:18.170 --> 00:49:24.220
is that the model is not as
sensitive to that aspect.

00:49:24.220 --> 00:49:33.520
And we want to steer the model
to not misbehave in some way.

00:49:33.520 --> 00:49:37.820
So here, for instance, the fact
of responding as, I actually

00:49:37.820 --> 00:49:41.100
do not suggest you do
anything, it's maybe something

00:49:41.100 --> 00:49:44.240
that the model would respond,
and you did your SFT correctly.

00:49:44.240 --> 00:49:48.260
But sometimes, the
model misbehaving

00:49:48.260 --> 00:49:51.020
actually tells you something
about maybe something

00:49:51.020 --> 00:49:54.960
that happened in the SFT
stage that was not intended.

00:49:54.960 --> 00:49:58.940
So maybe you fed it
data that actually you

00:49:58.940 --> 00:50:00.860
were not intending to do so.

00:50:00.860 --> 00:50:05.660
The misbehaving
parts is also a stage

00:50:05.660 --> 00:50:09.540
where, looking at your SFT
data and just making sure

00:50:09.540 --> 00:50:13.460
that you have trained your
model on data that you intended,

00:50:13.460 --> 00:50:15.980
with the quality
that you intended,

00:50:15.980 --> 00:50:19.740
it's a good time
to do that there.

00:50:19.740 --> 00:50:22.040
So how do you collect
preference data?

00:50:22.040 --> 00:50:25.080
So you can either have a
pointwise scoring system--

00:50:25.080 --> 00:50:28.100
so you have a prompt and then
you have several answers.

00:50:28.100 --> 00:50:28.880
This one is good.

00:50:28.880 --> 00:50:31.140
This one is not good
on an absolute scale.

00:50:31.140 --> 00:50:34.540
But we said that this is
kind of hard to do as humans,

00:50:34.540 --> 00:50:39.180
to have an absolute judgment.

00:50:39.180 --> 00:50:44.140
You can have this pairwise
preference data, which

00:50:44.140 --> 00:50:46.380
is exactly what
we just mentioned,

00:50:46.380 --> 00:50:50.700
where we have two
responses, and then we

00:50:50.700 --> 00:50:53.420
say which one is
better than the other.

00:50:53.420 --> 00:50:55.000
So this is the pairwise setting.

00:50:55.000 --> 00:50:57.200
And then the listwise
setting is you have a prompt

00:50:57.200 --> 00:51:00.540
and then you have a list
of possible answers.

00:51:00.540 --> 00:51:03.300
And you just rank
those from, let's say,

00:51:03.300 --> 00:51:06.220
most preferred to
least preferred way.

00:51:06.220 --> 00:51:12.180
For simplicity purposes,
most of the time, people

00:51:12.180 --> 00:51:15.300
collect pairwise
data when it comes

00:51:15.300 --> 00:51:19.790
to quantifying what kind of
preference you want to inject.

00:51:19.790 --> 00:51:23.660
So the next question
is, how can you

00:51:23.660 --> 00:51:27.390
know, for data that
you don't know outside,

00:51:27.390 --> 00:51:34.570
how can you know that a given
response is good or bad?

00:51:34.570 --> 00:51:37.470
How can you generalize that?

00:51:37.470 --> 00:51:44.790
And this is what is
motivating the first step

00:51:44.790 --> 00:51:48.910
that we're going to talk about,
which is called reward modeling.

00:51:48.910 --> 00:51:52.270
So here what we want
to do is to build

00:51:52.270 --> 00:51:58.750
a model that can tell us whether
a response to a given prompt

00:51:58.750 --> 00:52:01.830
is good or not.

00:52:01.830 --> 00:52:07.830
If we have such a model, then
the idea in the second stage

00:52:07.830 --> 00:52:15.920
is to align our model to align
with good behavior, which

00:52:15.920 --> 00:52:18.720
is quantified by
our reward model

00:52:18.720 --> 00:52:22.680
actually outputting good
scores for some answers

00:52:22.680 --> 00:52:31.000
and avoiding outputs that are
associated with lower scores.

00:52:31.000 --> 00:52:36.920
And this is where RLHF
comes into the picture.

00:52:36.920 --> 00:52:39.880
So who has heard of RLHF?

00:52:39.880 --> 00:52:40.740
Yeah, a lot of you.

00:52:40.740 --> 00:52:45.360
So RLHF stands for Reinforcement
Learning from Human Feedback.

00:52:45.360 --> 00:52:52.520
And it is a method to align your
model with human preferences.

00:52:52.520 --> 00:52:55.560
So why is it called RLHF?

00:52:55.560 --> 00:52:58.520
Because in the
first stage, where

00:52:58.520 --> 00:53:03.160
you want to learn how to
distinguish good from bad,

00:53:03.160 --> 00:53:10.120
you are training your reward
model on human preference data.

00:53:10.120 --> 00:53:13.610
So this is where the human
preference gets modeled.

00:53:13.610 --> 00:53:16.770
And the second stage is
to use the model that

00:53:16.770 --> 00:53:21.890
was trained on human preferences
in order to align your model

00:53:21.890 --> 00:53:25.170
to behave better.

00:53:25.170 --> 00:53:28.250
So that's the idea.

00:53:28.250 --> 00:53:33.330
So one thing that I will
say is that RLHF, and just,

00:53:33.330 --> 00:53:37.370
in general, reinforcement
learning-based methods,

00:53:37.370 --> 00:53:43.290
they are quite technical, which
is the reason why, today, we'll

00:53:43.290 --> 00:53:47.330
focus first and foremost
on the intuition.

00:53:47.330 --> 00:53:51.050
And we're keeping all
the mathematical part

00:53:51.050 --> 00:53:53.250
for next lecture.

00:53:53.250 --> 00:53:55.570
So I hope next lecture
you'll come prepared.

00:53:55.570 --> 00:53:56.590
We'll do some math.

00:53:56.590 --> 00:54:01.050
But this lecture, we will
focus more on the intuition.

00:54:01.050 --> 00:54:03.170
And the reason why we're
distinguishing the two

00:54:03.170 --> 00:54:05.490
is because it's actually
quite technical,

00:54:05.490 --> 00:54:08.270
and there are some
details to take care of.

00:54:11.490 --> 00:54:16.260
With that being said, let's
now go through the two steps

00:54:16.260 --> 00:54:19.340
that we mentioned-- so reward
modeling and reinforcement

00:54:19.340 --> 00:54:20.660
learning.

00:54:20.660 --> 00:54:27.220
So reward modeling, our goal
is to learn how to distinguish

00:54:27.220 --> 00:54:31.740
good answers from bad answers.

00:54:31.740 --> 00:54:36.980
And here, given a prompt
and given some answer,

00:54:36.980 --> 00:54:39.300
we want our model
to say, yes, it's

00:54:39.300 --> 00:54:42.740
a good answer to that prompt,
or, no, it's not a good answer.

00:54:42.740 --> 00:54:44.700
So that is our goal.

00:54:44.700 --> 00:54:48.500
And in order to do
that, people typically

00:54:48.500 --> 00:54:54.440
use a formulation that
is quite specific.

00:54:54.440 --> 00:54:57.260
It's called the
Bradley-Terry formulation.

00:54:57.260 --> 00:55:00.660
And it is a
formulation that models

00:55:00.660 --> 00:55:04.580
the probability of something
being preferred over something

00:55:04.580 --> 00:55:07.280
else in a very specific manner.

00:55:07.280 --> 00:55:10.620
So here it's probability
of, let's say,

00:55:10.620 --> 00:55:14.060
yi being preferred
over yj, which

00:55:14.060 --> 00:55:20.500
is equal to the
sigmoid of ri minus rj.

00:55:20.500 --> 00:55:26.300
So we're just saying that yi,
there is some reward ri that

00:55:26.300 --> 00:55:29.180
characterizes it, yj
there is some reward

00:55:29.180 --> 00:55:31.340
rj that characterizes it.

00:55:31.340 --> 00:55:33.820
What we're saying
is the probability

00:55:33.820 --> 00:55:39.460
of yi being preferred over yj
is the sigmoid of the difference

00:55:39.460 --> 00:55:41.780
between the two rewards.

00:55:41.780 --> 00:55:45.060
That is what we're saying.

00:55:45.060 --> 00:55:46.820
So here, as a reminder--
so I'm not sure

00:55:46.820 --> 00:55:48.862
if you're all familiar
with the sigmoid function.

00:55:48.862 --> 00:55:51.820
The sigmoid function
is equal to 1 over 1

00:55:51.820 --> 00:55:53.940
plus exponential of minus x.

00:55:53.940 --> 00:55:57.600
So it has the shape
and the slides.

00:55:57.600 --> 00:56:00.340
So when it tends to
minus infinity, it's 0,

00:56:00.340 --> 00:56:05.060
and over plus infinity, it is 1.

00:56:05.060 --> 00:56:09.230
And the way we're going
to train our reward model

00:56:09.230 --> 00:56:13.470
is we're going to leverage
the pairwise preference

00:56:13.470 --> 00:56:15.090
data that we have collected.

00:56:15.090 --> 00:56:16.830
So if you remember,
we had mentioned

00:56:16.830 --> 00:56:20.670
that in order to
collect preference data,

00:56:20.670 --> 00:56:25.050
pairwise was the
easiest way to do that.

00:56:25.050 --> 00:56:31.990
So we have for each prompt a
winning response and a losing

00:56:31.990 --> 00:56:33.830
response.

00:56:33.830 --> 00:56:36.290
So let's assume we
have a prompt x.

00:56:36.290 --> 00:56:41.150
And then we have a response
yw, winning response,

00:56:41.150 --> 00:56:45.710
and a losing response yl.

00:56:45.710 --> 00:56:50.810
And our goal is to train our
reward model in order to say,

00:56:50.810 --> 00:56:53.870
well, the winning is
indeed the high reward

00:56:53.870 --> 00:56:56.950
and the losing is
a lower reward.

00:56:56.950 --> 00:57:00.150
So the way we train
our reward model

00:57:00.150 --> 00:57:05.840
is by using a pairwise loss.

00:57:05.840 --> 00:57:10.900
So for a given prompt,
we would put yw and yl.

00:57:10.900 --> 00:57:14.160
We would compute the
reward through our model.

00:57:14.160 --> 00:57:19.240
And then we would update
the weights of the model

00:57:19.240 --> 00:57:22.920
by using this pairwise loss.

00:57:22.920 --> 00:57:27.660
So this pairwise loss may
be a little bit scary.

00:57:27.660 --> 00:57:33.120
So I'm going to
just motivate that.

00:57:33.120 --> 00:57:38.040
So if you remember for
cross-entropy, what we said was,

00:57:38.040 --> 00:57:43.120
intuitively, you want to
learn parameters theta that

00:57:43.120 --> 00:57:48.800
maximize the probability of your
model seeing the training set.

00:57:48.800 --> 00:57:55.900
And so here the event that
you want to model is yw--

00:57:55.900 --> 00:57:58.640
so winning response
being preferred

00:57:58.640 --> 00:58:02.000
over a losing response.

00:58:02.000 --> 00:58:08.690
So here what you want to
maximizes the probability

00:58:08.690 --> 00:58:14.210
that your model says
that, let's say,

00:58:14.210 --> 00:58:19.290
winning response is preferred
over a losing response, roughly

00:58:19.290 --> 00:58:22.130
speaking.

00:58:22.130 --> 00:58:25.050
So if you remember,
so that piece

00:58:25.050 --> 00:58:30.450
the Bradley-Terry formulation,
what does it tell us?

00:58:30.450 --> 00:58:36.730
It is equal to sigma
of r theta, which

00:58:36.730 --> 00:58:40.730
is your reward model,
of-- so your prompts

00:58:40.730 --> 00:58:46.890
and the winning answer minus
the reward model of your prompt

00:58:46.890 --> 00:58:48.110
and the losing answer.

00:58:50.690 --> 00:58:53.130
So this is what the
Bradley-Terry formulation

00:58:53.130 --> 00:58:55.570
tells us.

00:58:55.570 --> 00:59:02.580
So you want to maximize
this across all your pairs.

00:59:02.580 --> 00:59:07.340
But as we said,
you like logarithm

00:59:07.340 --> 00:59:09.000
because it is monotonic.

00:59:09.000 --> 00:59:12.220
And these quantities
can be very small

00:59:12.220 --> 00:59:13.800
and can lead to instability.

00:59:13.800 --> 00:59:16.717
So you take the log.

00:59:16.717 --> 00:59:18.300
And actually, in the
machine learning,

00:59:18.300 --> 00:59:21.240
you like to minimize things as
opposed to maximizing things.

00:59:21.240 --> 00:59:23.780
So here you want
to maximize this,

00:59:23.780 --> 00:59:27.700
maximizing the log of this,
which is the same as minimizing

00:59:27.700 --> 00:59:31.940
minus the log of this.

00:59:31.940 --> 00:59:36.500
And if you look at the slide,
it's exactly what is written.

00:59:36.500 --> 00:59:41.220
So you want to
minimize minus log

00:59:41.220 --> 00:59:47.340
of sigma of reward of winning
minus reward of losing.

00:59:47.340 --> 00:59:52.140
That is just a result of the
Bradley-Terry formulation.

00:59:52.140 --> 00:59:53.720
So let's assume you do that.

00:59:53.720 --> 00:59:56.160
So in order to-- yeah?

00:59:56.160 --> 00:59:57.640
Yeah, the question
is, what is e?

00:59:57.640 --> 00:59:59.680
Yes, it's the expectation
over your whole data.

00:59:59.680 --> 01:00:03.572
So you can think of it as a
sum or an average if you want.

01:00:03.572 --> 01:00:06.030
So the question is, what is
the architecture of your reward

01:00:06.030 --> 01:00:06.610
model?

01:00:06.610 --> 01:00:09.670
Well, there's been
several ways people

01:00:09.670 --> 01:00:11.830
have been modeling rewards.

01:00:11.830 --> 01:00:17.550
One way is to leverage
the backbone of your LLM.

01:00:17.550 --> 01:00:22.910
But your LLM is trained in a
next-token-prediction fashion.

01:00:22.910 --> 01:00:27.430
So what you can do is
if you put some inputs,

01:00:27.430 --> 01:00:32.230
you can take the embedding
representation corresponding

01:00:32.230 --> 01:00:35.550
to the last token that
was input and project that

01:00:35.550 --> 01:00:36.410
onto some space.

01:00:36.410 --> 01:00:37.693
You can do that.

01:00:37.693 --> 01:00:39.110
Another way you
can do that is you

01:00:39.110 --> 01:00:40.730
can use an encoder-only model.

01:00:40.730 --> 01:00:44.150
So you put everything,
self-attention, and just project

01:00:44.150 --> 01:00:45.410
on the data.

01:00:45.410 --> 01:00:47.070
So the first method,
the good thing

01:00:47.070 --> 01:00:51.350
is that you can leverage the
backbone that you already have.

01:00:51.350 --> 01:00:52.890
So yeah, you can do either way.

01:00:52.890 --> 01:00:57.270
But typically, you can
do one of these two.

01:00:57.270 --> 01:00:59.470
OK, cool.

01:00:59.470 --> 01:01:03.630
So the label here
is human preference

01:01:03.630 --> 01:01:06.130
because you need that in
order to say it's RLHF.

01:01:08.710 --> 01:01:12.270
And actually, I
just realized, yeah,

01:01:12.270 --> 01:01:13.615
this was exactly my next slide.

01:01:13.615 --> 01:01:14.990
So the kind of
model would either

01:01:14.990 --> 01:01:18.270
be a backbone of LLM
with the classification

01:01:18.270 --> 01:01:23.510
head on the last token
that you want to leverage

01:01:23.510 --> 01:01:25.210
or an encoder-only model.

01:01:28.350 --> 01:01:31.710
Cool, OK, so that
first stage, you

01:01:31.710 --> 01:01:35.510
went from just having
preference pairs

01:01:35.510 --> 01:01:40.270
and just wanting to learn good
responses from bad responses

01:01:40.270 --> 01:01:42.630
to actually having a
model that tells you,

01:01:42.630 --> 01:01:45.230
for a given prompt
and a given response,

01:01:45.230 --> 01:01:49.070
whether that is actually a good
response or a bad response.

01:01:49.070 --> 01:01:52.830
So now the second stage, which
is the more mathematically

01:01:52.830 --> 01:01:58.680
involved stage, is the
reinforcement learning stage.

01:01:58.680 --> 01:02:01.400
And here the purpose
of that stage

01:02:01.400 --> 01:02:04.640
is to change the
weights of your model

01:02:04.640 --> 01:02:08.480
so that it aligns with the
rewards that are mentioned

01:02:08.480 --> 01:02:10.960
by that reward model.

01:02:10.960 --> 01:02:14.080
So how would you do that?

01:02:14.080 --> 01:02:19.800
So here, you would have a
prompt and ask your model

01:02:19.800 --> 01:02:23.200
to generate a response.

01:02:23.200 --> 01:02:27.040
And here, you want to know if
this is the kind of behavior you

01:02:27.040 --> 01:02:29.440
want to promote or demote.

01:02:29.440 --> 01:02:31.840
So here what you
would do is you would

01:02:31.840 --> 01:02:34.800
input your input
prompts and the response

01:02:34.800 --> 01:02:37.080
into your reward model--

01:02:37.080 --> 01:02:40.480
let's suppose it says,
no, it's a bad response--

01:02:40.480 --> 01:02:49.560
and use that judgment to
update the weights of your LLM.

01:02:49.560 --> 01:02:51.560
So there's a lot
of math involved.

01:02:51.560 --> 01:02:54.873
But long story short,
the reward model

01:02:54.873 --> 01:02:56.290
is something that
you have trained

01:02:56.290 --> 01:02:59.050
in stage 1 that is now frozen.

01:02:59.050 --> 01:03:01.330
The weights that you're
changing are the weights

01:03:01.330 --> 01:03:03.610
of your LLM, which
is the LLM that you

01:03:03.610 --> 01:03:07.650
want to align the behavior for.

01:03:07.650 --> 01:03:13.290
And the way you do that is by
using an objective function that

01:03:13.290 --> 01:03:16.790
is typically composed
of two parts.

01:03:16.790 --> 01:03:21.690
The first part is trying
to maximize the rewards,

01:03:21.690 --> 01:03:27.150
but also to not deviate too
much from the reference model,

01:03:27.150 --> 01:03:30.630
because, similar to my
earlier comment with LoRA,

01:03:30.630 --> 01:03:33.850
with respect to the
pretrained model,

01:03:33.850 --> 01:03:35.810
your model here is
already good enough.

01:03:35.810 --> 01:03:38.350
It knows how to
respond to questions.

01:03:38.350 --> 01:03:40.030
It knows a bunch of things.

01:03:40.030 --> 01:03:44.330
You don't want to change
your weights too much.

01:03:44.330 --> 01:03:48.610
And the thing is, your
reward model is not perfect.

01:03:48.610 --> 01:03:52.660
Sometimes it will say, oh,
it's bad, whereas it's good.

01:03:52.660 --> 01:03:57.020
So you don't want your model to
go too far from your reference

01:03:57.020 --> 01:03:58.020
model.

01:03:58.020 --> 01:03:59.740
So you typically have
an additional term

01:03:59.740 --> 01:04:03.100
that penalizes too big of
a difference with respect

01:04:03.100 --> 01:04:05.580
to the reference.

01:04:05.580 --> 01:04:08.020
So here, typically, the
number of observations

01:04:08.020 --> 01:04:12.220
is orders of magnitude
higher than the reward model.

01:04:12.220 --> 01:04:15.340
And you would initialize
your model at the SFT model

01:04:15.340 --> 01:04:17.700
that you obtained previously.

01:04:17.700 --> 01:04:19.900
And you would have an
objective function, which

01:04:19.900 --> 01:04:25.480
is exactly what I mentioned, a
trade-off between two pieces,

01:04:25.480 --> 01:04:27.820
which are maximizing
the rewards,

01:04:27.820 --> 01:04:31.500
but also not deviating too
much from the base model.

01:04:31.500 --> 01:04:35.860
And there's actually a key term
that I want to mention here.

01:04:35.860 --> 01:04:39.440
People sometimes
say reward hacking.

01:04:39.440 --> 01:04:42.700
So reward hacking is the
fact or the phenomenon

01:04:42.700 --> 01:04:48.020
of your model trying to please
the reward model by going

01:04:48.020 --> 01:04:52.750
into the direction that the
reward model likes without this

01:04:52.750 --> 01:04:55.710
being necessarily a
good thing, if you just

01:04:55.710 --> 01:04:59.230
forget about your reward
model from your perspective,

01:04:59.230 --> 01:05:04.210
just because the reward
model is not perfect.

01:05:04.210 --> 01:05:04.710
Yeah?

01:05:07.510 --> 01:05:08.490
It's a great question.

01:05:08.490 --> 01:05:12.290
So your question is, how do we
update the weights of the model?

01:05:12.290 --> 01:05:14.730
And we'll need to go
into details for that.

01:05:14.730 --> 01:05:17.510
So save your question
for next week.

01:05:17.510 --> 01:05:19.510
We'll go into that in detail.

01:05:19.510 --> 01:05:24.490
But the one thing I want you to
remember here is in this stage,

01:05:24.490 --> 01:05:25.770
the reward model is fixed.

01:05:25.770 --> 01:05:27.370
We're not updating its weights.

01:05:27.370 --> 01:05:31.370
We're only updating
the weights of the LLM.

01:05:31.370 --> 01:05:33.650
And we will see next week
exactly how we do that.

01:05:37.310 --> 01:05:38.990
Cool.

01:05:38.990 --> 01:05:41.190
So we just saw RLHF.

01:05:41.190 --> 01:05:46.230
You need to train a reward
model and then do RL on your LLM

01:05:46.230 --> 01:05:48.350
in order to update
its weights in order

01:05:48.350 --> 01:05:50.670
to align the behavior
with what you want.

01:05:50.670 --> 01:05:54.510
The problem is,
RL is complicated.

01:05:54.510 --> 01:05:56.950
You need a reward model.

01:05:56.950 --> 01:06:00.750
A lot of attention is needed
to select the hyperparameters

01:06:00.750 --> 01:06:04.590
and so on, which is why
people have tried to formulate

01:06:04.590 --> 01:06:08.830
this problem in a
supervised manner, which

01:06:08.830 --> 01:06:14.190
is the idea behind DPO, which
stands for Direct Preference

01:06:14.190 --> 01:06:15.950
Optimization.

01:06:15.950 --> 01:06:18.110
So we will not go
into the math here,

01:06:18.110 --> 01:06:22.110
but I just want to tell you
that the loss function that

01:06:22.110 --> 01:06:24.710
is derived is something
that is actually

01:06:24.710 --> 01:06:28.150
very similar to
the loss function

01:06:28.150 --> 01:06:31.110
that we obtained for
the reward model.

01:06:31.110 --> 01:06:36.990
If you assume that r is
equal to some quantity

01:06:36.990 --> 01:06:41.550
times the logarithm of the
probability from the model

01:06:41.550 --> 01:06:46.760
that you're trying to tweak
over the reference model,

01:06:46.760 --> 01:06:49.320
and if you see
that, you will also

01:06:49.320 --> 01:06:55.440
see that this loss function
incentivizes your model

01:06:55.440 --> 01:07:01.360
to increase the probability of
generating the winning answer

01:07:01.360 --> 01:07:03.680
compared to the reference.

01:07:03.680 --> 01:07:08.080
And it incentivizes
your model to decrease

01:07:08.080 --> 01:07:11.800
the probability of generating
a losing answer compared

01:07:11.800 --> 01:07:15.160
to the reference.

01:07:15.160 --> 01:07:19.560
So I'll go through
that super quickly.

01:07:19.560 --> 01:07:24.720
So here what we're
saying is something,

01:07:24.720 --> 01:07:34.760
let's say, minus expectation of,
let's say, log of your sigmoids,

01:07:34.760 --> 01:07:40.820
and then you have this
huge thing, like minus rl--

01:07:43.360 --> 01:07:51.050
so your loss is such
that you want rw minus rl

01:07:51.050 --> 01:07:54.530
to be as big as possible.

01:07:54.530 --> 01:07:56.110
So what does that mean?

01:07:56.110 --> 01:08:02.090
So if you say r is equal
to some quantity times

01:08:02.090 --> 01:08:08.930
log of probability from the
model that you're tweaking to,

01:08:08.930 --> 01:08:12.690
let's say, generate an
answer over probability

01:08:12.690 --> 01:08:17.850
of your reference model
to generate an answer--

01:08:17.850 --> 01:08:22.890
so what this is saying is
in order for the difference

01:08:22.890 --> 01:08:27.450
to be big, which is what
your loss is trying to do,

01:08:27.450 --> 01:08:31.830
it will try to make
rw as big as possible.

01:08:31.830 --> 01:08:33.290
So how can I do that?

01:08:33.290 --> 01:08:37.130
It is by having the
probability of the model

01:08:37.130 --> 01:08:40.330
we're trying to tweak be
higher than the probability

01:08:40.330 --> 01:08:45.939
of your reference model for
that output given the inputs.

01:08:45.939 --> 01:08:49.380
So this means that
we're trying to increase

01:08:49.380 --> 01:08:55.899
the probability of the winning
answer being generated.

01:08:55.899 --> 01:08:58.359
And what you want to
do is to decrease rl.

01:08:58.359 --> 01:09:00.020
How do you decrease rl?

01:09:00.020 --> 01:09:03.580
You actually decrease
the probability

01:09:03.580 --> 01:09:10.500
of your model generating that
losing answer given your input.

01:09:10.500 --> 01:09:12.180
Yes?

01:09:12.180 --> 01:09:13.680
So the question is, what is pi?

01:09:13.680 --> 01:09:15.620
So here pi is--

01:09:15.620 --> 01:09:19.700
we will see next week
that this model LLM,

01:09:19.700 --> 01:09:21.899
we will call it a policy.

01:09:21.899 --> 01:09:24.420
And when you talk about
policy, you typically

01:09:24.420 --> 01:09:27.160
talk about its predicted
probability in terms of pi.

01:09:27.160 --> 01:09:30.390
So yeah, so this
notation is rl-specific.

01:09:30.390 --> 01:09:31.640
And yeah-- to your point, yes.

01:09:34.700 --> 01:09:35.939
OK, great.

01:09:35.939 --> 01:09:39.720
I just want to say that
this formula can be scary.

01:09:39.720 --> 01:09:43.109
But if you take the
Bradley-Terry formulation

01:09:43.109 --> 01:09:47.149
in mind and interpret
the left and right term

01:09:47.149 --> 01:09:50.910
as a function of the
ratio between the model

01:09:50.910 --> 01:09:54.930
that you're trying to tweak
over the reference model,

01:09:54.930 --> 01:09:57.950
if you think about
that this way,

01:09:57.950 --> 01:10:01.150
then, hopefully, it
makes a bit more sense.

01:10:01.150 --> 01:10:02.750
Yeah?

01:10:02.750 --> 01:10:04.750
So yeah, the question is,
is the reference model

01:10:04.750 --> 01:10:06.650
the supervised fine-tuned model?

01:10:06.650 --> 01:10:10.190
Yes, it is the model that
you start from when you

01:10:10.190 --> 01:10:12.390
perform that training, yeah.

01:10:14.990 --> 01:10:16.150
OK, cool.

01:10:16.150 --> 01:10:22.310
Moving on, so you will see and
hear RLHF and DPO out there.

01:10:22.310 --> 01:10:25.910
I just want to tell you a little
bit about the differences.

01:10:25.910 --> 01:10:30.350
So RLHF, as we mentioned,
is composed of two stages.

01:10:30.350 --> 01:10:33.390
The first stage is
reward modeling.

01:10:33.390 --> 01:10:36.350
The second stage is
reinforcement learning.

01:10:36.350 --> 01:10:43.470
So there is models to train,
and it can be compute-heavy,

01:10:43.470 --> 01:10:49.030
whereas DPO is a supervised
learning way to do that.

01:10:49.030 --> 01:10:55.990
But one thing with
DPO is it is not

01:10:55.990 --> 01:10:59.790
penalizing the loss
based on outputs

01:10:59.790 --> 01:11:01.250
that the model is producing.

01:11:01.250 --> 01:11:03.550
It is actually on fixed outputs.

01:11:03.550 --> 01:11:06.830
So to your point, this
problem is still present here.

01:11:06.830 --> 01:11:09.390
And we will see that
in a few slides,

01:11:09.390 --> 01:11:13.470
that RLHF is an
on-policy method,

01:11:13.470 --> 01:11:17.710
because it is actually
leveraging the actual outputs

01:11:17.710 --> 01:11:23.510
from the LLM, which contrasts
with off-policy methods, which

01:11:23.510 --> 01:11:28.630
are actually training the model
based on fixed outputs that

01:11:28.630 --> 01:11:33.870
are not necessarily
generated by the model.

01:11:33.870 --> 01:11:37.680
So with that, going
back to our example,

01:11:37.680 --> 01:11:40.520
now let's assume we
have the same inputs.

01:11:40.520 --> 01:11:41.780
Now it's pretrained.

01:11:41.780 --> 01:11:43.540
It's instruction fine-tuned.

01:11:43.540 --> 01:11:45.800
And it's also preference-tuned.

01:11:45.800 --> 01:11:50.960
Now we will have a response that
is maybe more friendly in tone.

01:11:50.960 --> 01:11:53.920
So here, "Can I put my
teddy bear in the washer?"

01:11:53.920 --> 01:11:56.760
Instead of saying, "No," we will
have something that is maybe

01:11:56.760 --> 01:11:57.460
more friendly.

01:11:57.460 --> 01:11:58.860
No, "It's better not to.

01:11:58.860 --> 01:12:00.520
Your teddy could get hurt!

01:12:00.520 --> 01:12:02.640
A gentle hand wash is safer."

01:12:02.640 --> 01:12:05.020
So you can use this
method to, let's say,

01:12:05.020 --> 01:12:08.280
change the tone as an example.

01:12:08.280 --> 01:12:10.960
So now I want to talk to
you about a topic that

01:12:10.960 --> 01:12:17.920
has increased in importance
in the past two years.

01:12:17.920 --> 01:12:20.600
And this is the
topic of reasoning,

01:12:20.600 --> 01:12:24.800
which I will define here in a
very simple way as the ability

01:12:24.800 --> 01:12:27.880
to solve a problem.

01:12:27.880 --> 01:12:32.520
So just to contrast prompts
that are nonreasoning versus

01:12:32.520 --> 01:12:36.850
reasoning-- so here, if you
ask a knowledge-based question,

01:12:36.850 --> 01:12:40.450
what is the course code for
Stanford's Transformers &amp; LLMs

01:12:40.450 --> 01:12:41.250
class?"

01:12:41.250 --> 01:12:45.010
Well, it's something that
you know or you don't know.

01:12:45.010 --> 01:12:47.730
It is different from,
let's say, a math question.

01:12:47.730 --> 01:12:51.510
Let's assume you ask, OK, so
your bear was born in 2020.

01:12:51.510 --> 01:12:53.210
How old is the bear now?

01:12:53.210 --> 01:12:56.370
You need to think a little
bit about what year we're in

01:12:56.370 --> 01:13:01.010
and what is the difference in
year in order to get the answer.

01:13:01.010 --> 01:13:04.130
So this right
example is the kind

01:13:04.130 --> 01:13:08.690
of prompts that we're
trying to improve the model

01:13:08.690 --> 01:13:11.250
performance on.

01:13:11.250 --> 01:13:17.010
And if you remember, at the
end of last week's lecture,

01:13:17.010 --> 01:13:21.970
we saw a prompt technique
called Chain of Thought that

01:13:21.970 --> 01:13:27.570
tried to elicit this
behavior from the model

01:13:27.570 --> 01:13:32.170
by incentivizing it to
reveal its reasoning chain

01:13:32.170 --> 01:13:33.440
through a few-shot examples.

01:13:33.440 --> 01:13:37.140
So here we saw that if
we told the LLM, well,

01:13:37.140 --> 01:13:41.500
let's say, on this example,
how old is the bear,

01:13:41.500 --> 01:13:42.900
so in order to
get to the answer,

01:13:42.900 --> 01:13:45.720
I want you to output this chain.

01:13:45.720 --> 01:13:48.940
Then the LLM can learn to
output a reasoning chain

01:13:48.940 --> 01:13:51.180
before the answer.

01:13:51.180 --> 01:13:54.940
The problem with
that method is we

01:13:54.940 --> 01:13:57.720
don't want to always
tell the LLM to do that.

01:13:57.720 --> 01:14:01.580
We want the LLM to
do that by itself.

01:14:01.580 --> 01:14:05.340
And this is exactly
what we will see here.

01:14:05.340 --> 01:14:13.320
So in 2024, in September
of 2024, OpenAI, I believe,

01:14:13.320 --> 01:14:20.620
was the first company to tell
about improved performance

01:14:20.620 --> 01:14:25.260
on math and coding tasks
using this technique.

01:14:25.260 --> 01:14:31.950
And shortly after that,
almost all other frontier labs

01:14:31.950 --> 01:14:38.970
also had models that
used the same strategy.

01:14:38.970 --> 01:14:41.010
And we will see exactly
what that strategy is.

01:14:43.830 --> 01:14:50.870
In particular, DeepSeek-R1 was
the first open-weight model

01:14:50.870 --> 01:14:57.790
in January of 2025 to
actually go through the how.

01:14:57.790 --> 01:15:00.990
So that paper is
probably the first paper

01:15:00.990 --> 01:15:05.170
that really explains how
reasoning models are trained.

01:15:05.170 --> 01:15:07.550
And we will see exactly
how we do that here.

01:15:07.550 --> 01:15:12.270
So if we were in,
let's say, 2025,

01:15:12.270 --> 01:15:15.790
the way you would
recognize a reasoning model

01:15:15.790 --> 01:15:19.370
is because it was written
everywhere on the UI.

01:15:19.370 --> 01:15:21.750
So here, an example
of OpenAI-- there

01:15:21.750 --> 01:15:24.490
was a model called
ChatGPT-5 Thinking.

01:15:24.490 --> 01:15:25.950
So here it's a hint.

01:15:25.950 --> 01:15:27.830
And then the reasoning
chain, because it

01:15:27.830 --> 01:15:32.580
thought for some seconds, it
used to output the actual chain,

01:15:32.580 --> 01:15:36.880
maybe in a summarized way, and
you would have also an option

01:15:36.880 --> 01:15:39.840
to think more.

01:15:39.840 --> 01:15:42.080
But fast-forward to
today-- these models

01:15:42.080 --> 01:15:44.880
are actually everywhere.

01:15:44.880 --> 01:15:47.760
Almost all models are
reasoning models these days.

01:15:47.760 --> 01:15:49.760
And so the only place
where, let's say,

01:15:49.760 --> 01:15:53.060
if you interact with OpenAI,
the ChatGPT and so on,

01:15:53.060 --> 01:15:56.760
the only place you would see
a hint of the model being

01:15:56.760 --> 01:15:59.540
a reasoning model is
the thinking effort.

01:16:02.400 --> 01:16:08.640
So one thing that I want to
say is that the reasoning chain

01:16:08.640 --> 01:16:11.720
is something that is
actually important

01:16:11.720 --> 01:16:18.640
because it encapsulates the
previous input that the user has

01:16:18.640 --> 01:16:20.680
mentioned to the model.

01:16:20.680 --> 01:16:23.860
And I'm not sure if
you've heard in the news,

01:16:23.860 --> 01:16:27.160
but a couple of
months ago, there

01:16:27.160 --> 01:16:34.000
was a paper that showed that,
for some third-party providers,

01:16:34.000 --> 01:16:38.360
you could actually
recover the full reasoning

01:16:38.360 --> 01:16:43.080
chain by playing with the API.

01:16:43.080 --> 01:16:47.280
And the reason why that
paper did a lot of noise

01:16:47.280 --> 01:16:50.160
is because these
reasoning chains,

01:16:50.160 --> 01:16:53.720
they can actually contain
a lot of information

01:16:53.720 --> 01:16:57.960
about the user, about the chat,
what was mentioned before,

01:16:57.960 --> 01:17:01.440
which is probably not something
that you want to expose,

01:17:01.440 --> 01:17:03.120
for safety reasons.

01:17:03.120 --> 01:17:07.680
But also, another reason is to
not have the reasoning chain

01:17:07.680 --> 01:17:11.800
revealed for other
people to train on it,

01:17:11.800 --> 01:17:16.120
because we will see that having
a high-quality reasoning chain

01:17:16.120 --> 01:17:20.160
can actually improve the
performance of your model--

01:17:20.160 --> 01:17:24.600
so just to say that in
general, the reasoning chain

01:17:24.600 --> 01:17:29.650
is something that people are
trying to protect, to not show,

01:17:29.650 --> 01:17:31.450
for these two reasons.

01:17:31.450 --> 01:17:33.370
And this is the
reason why you would

01:17:33.370 --> 01:17:37.850
see the UI that has changed
quite a bit between 2025 5

01:17:37.850 --> 01:17:41.010
and now, because right
now, actually don't really

01:17:41.010 --> 01:17:45.850
get the reasoning chain out
of the box like you used to.

01:17:45.850 --> 01:17:52.850
So the idea is to
incentivize the model

01:17:52.850 --> 01:17:58.890
to actually output a reasoning
chain before getting its answer.

01:17:58.890 --> 01:18:03.970
So just on the what is
what, the reasoning chain

01:18:03.970 --> 01:18:07.410
is actually considered
as a model output.

01:18:07.410 --> 01:18:09.590
So if you think
about output token,

01:18:09.590 --> 01:18:14.970
you actually need to consider
the reasoning as part of it.

01:18:14.970 --> 01:18:19.070
So if you were to consider
using this reasoning models,

01:18:19.070 --> 01:18:22.210
and if you have a
finite budget, just

01:18:22.210 --> 01:18:27.140
keep in mind that you're being
charged for both what you see,

01:18:27.140 --> 01:18:29.540
but also, the typically
hidden reasoning

01:18:29.540 --> 01:18:32.460
chain that the model produces.

01:18:32.460 --> 01:18:35.100
And so here I just
highlighted in the slides

01:18:35.100 --> 01:18:38.960
the parts in the main providers
where this is mentioned.

01:18:38.960 --> 01:18:40.260
Yeah?

01:18:40.260 --> 01:18:40.780
Yep.

01:18:40.780 --> 01:18:42.700
So the question is,
can the model decide

01:18:42.700 --> 01:18:44.480
on what to reason versus not?

01:18:44.480 --> 01:18:47.420
Yes, typically, it
has a way to decide.

01:18:47.420 --> 01:18:49.460
It's part of the
training process.

01:18:49.460 --> 01:18:52.300
You as a user can also
set that, typically

01:18:52.300 --> 01:18:54.500
by some toggle thinking effort.

01:18:54.500 --> 01:18:56.500
But even within that,
typically, models

01:18:56.500 --> 01:19:01.020
are trained to categorize
prompts that are maybe

01:19:01.020 --> 01:19:03.660
worth less effort versus more.

01:19:03.660 --> 01:19:04.920
So short answer is yes.

01:19:07.500 --> 01:19:10.700
So moving on, the
main question now

01:19:10.700 --> 01:19:12.900
is how we can train
such a model, how

01:19:12.900 --> 01:19:16.820
we can incentivize a model
to output a reasoning

01:19:16.820 --> 01:19:18.940
chain before its answer.

01:19:18.940 --> 01:19:26.310
So the idea is to train
our model on rewards,

01:19:26.310 --> 01:19:28.070
but this time, they're
not the rewards

01:19:28.070 --> 01:19:30.330
that we saw a few slides ago.

01:19:30.330 --> 01:19:33.990
They are actually
verifiable, because when

01:19:33.990 --> 01:19:38.630
it comes to math
and coding problems,

01:19:38.630 --> 01:19:43.150
when you get an answer, you
can tell if the answer is

01:19:43.150 --> 01:19:44.963
right or wrong.

01:19:44.963 --> 01:19:46.130
So I'll give you an example.

01:19:46.130 --> 01:19:48.470
Let's assume you
do a coding task.

01:19:48.470 --> 01:19:50.030
You have a solution.

01:19:50.030 --> 01:19:52.110
How do you know if
your solution works?

01:19:52.110 --> 01:19:55.350
Well, you run through
some test cases.

01:19:55.350 --> 01:20:00.030
Let's assume you want to
resolve a math problem.

01:20:00.030 --> 01:20:01.910
How do you know if it's correct?

01:20:01.910 --> 01:20:06.990
Well, you just compare your
answer with the ground truth?

01:20:06.990 --> 01:20:14.230
So the nice thing
with this setup

01:20:14.230 --> 01:20:18.920
is that for every
model answer, you

01:20:18.920 --> 01:20:24.400
can tell if your model
had it right or wrong.

01:20:24.400 --> 01:20:32.920
So you can design a reward that
relies on the final answer,

01:20:32.920 --> 01:20:36.400
and also on the fact
that the answer needs

01:20:36.400 --> 01:20:40.400
to contain a reasoning chain.

01:20:40.400 --> 01:20:42.440
And so here you
can actually have

01:20:42.440 --> 01:20:47.440
a reward that is composed
of the answer being correct

01:20:47.440 --> 01:20:51.920
and the answer containing a
reasoning chain-- so typically

01:20:51.920 --> 01:20:57.080
having reasoning tokens.

01:20:57.080 --> 01:20:59.760
And the way you would
train your model

01:20:59.760 --> 01:21:03.900
would be using rl algorithms.

01:21:03.900 --> 01:21:07.640
So we will see next week
in-depth one such algorithm

01:21:07.640 --> 01:21:11.880
that has grown in popularity
that is called GRPO.

01:21:11.880 --> 01:21:14.925
That stands for Group
Relative Policy optimization.

01:21:14.925 --> 01:21:15.800
Don't worry the math.

01:21:15.800 --> 01:21:16.780
We'll see it next week.

01:21:16.780 --> 01:21:20.220
But at a very high level, what
we're doing is given a query,

01:21:20.220 --> 01:21:22.860
given a prompt, we're
telling our LLM,

01:21:22.860 --> 01:21:28.480
which is here, the policy,
to output several responses.

01:21:28.480 --> 01:21:32.920
And we figure out if these
responses are correct or not.

01:21:32.920 --> 01:21:37.480
And what we do is we
compute advantages,

01:21:37.480 --> 01:21:44.360
which is how much better a given
answer is relative to the group.

01:21:44.360 --> 01:21:50.180
If it's much better, let's
have the model do more of that.

01:21:50.180 --> 01:21:53.400
If not, let's have the
model do less of that,

01:21:53.400 --> 01:21:58.120
and still by penalizing with
respect to a reference model.

01:21:58.120 --> 01:22:03.520
So again, the objective
is maximize the advantages

01:22:03.520 --> 01:22:06.860
while not deviating too
much from the base model.

01:22:06.860 --> 01:22:09.800
So here it's the same mindset.

01:22:09.800 --> 01:22:13.000
The main difference
here is you're actually

01:22:13.000 --> 01:22:15.390
relying on verifiable rewards.

01:22:15.390 --> 01:22:17.250
You don't need a reward model.

01:22:17.250 --> 01:22:20.210
So here you're just
using the knowledge

01:22:20.210 --> 01:22:24.930
that you have on the
ground truth of a problem

01:22:24.930 --> 01:22:29.450
and comparing whether the answer
that is output by the model

01:22:29.450 --> 01:22:31.670
is matching the ground truth.

01:22:36.370 --> 01:22:38.370
Does that make sense?

01:22:38.370 --> 01:22:43.890
So again, I want to emphasize
next week we'll see the details.

01:22:43.890 --> 01:22:48.970
If you do that, then you will
see that your model actually

01:22:48.970 --> 01:22:52.890
gets better and better at
these reasoning problems.

01:22:52.890 --> 01:22:55.290
So on the left side,
we have the performance

01:22:55.290 --> 01:22:58.210
of the model as a
function of the rl step.

01:22:58.210 --> 01:23:01.605
So it's a measure of how far
you are in the training process.

01:23:01.605 --> 01:23:03.230
So here it's getting
better and better.

01:23:03.230 --> 01:23:07.370
And on the right side, you
have the empirical length

01:23:07.370 --> 01:23:12.750
of the reasoning chain,
which gets increasingly high.

01:23:15.760 --> 01:23:17.940
I think I already told
you, but lecture 4

01:23:17.940 --> 01:23:19.820
is where we'll talk
about the details.

01:23:19.820 --> 01:23:22.680
So here, if we have
some reasoning problem,

01:23:22.680 --> 01:23:25.580
we tell the model,
well, now, the model

01:23:25.580 --> 01:23:32.420
can answer in a way that
aligns with what we want.

01:23:32.420 --> 01:23:34.300
There is something,
before giving it

01:23:34.300 --> 01:23:36.580
to Shervine, that I want
to talk to you about.

01:23:36.580 --> 01:23:38.460
There is a limitation,
core limitation

01:23:38.460 --> 01:23:41.940
of this approach, which
is that you're actually

01:23:41.940 --> 01:23:46.900
getting a very coarse reward.

01:23:46.900 --> 01:23:50.300
You're getting a reward
for your whole output.

01:23:50.300 --> 01:23:53.380
It's either the whole
output is correct

01:23:53.380 --> 01:23:56.820
or the whole output
is incorrect.

01:23:56.820 --> 01:24:00.740
So that is one problem.

01:24:00.740 --> 01:24:05.180
The second problem is that
the methods that we saw that

01:24:05.180 --> 01:24:11.470
had a token-level signal,
they had the limitation

01:24:11.470 --> 01:24:14.950
of being based on sequences
that were actually not

01:24:14.950 --> 01:24:16.430
produced by the model.

01:24:16.430 --> 01:24:19.130
So we call such
sequences off-policy.

01:24:19.130 --> 01:24:21.670
So it's an off-policy method.

01:24:21.670 --> 01:24:23.990
So it's a dense
signal, but it's not

01:24:23.990 --> 01:24:27.750
produced by the model, which
can deviate from actually what

01:24:27.750 --> 01:24:31.350
the model is generating.

01:24:31.350 --> 01:24:32.710
So we have plus.

01:24:32.710 --> 01:24:35.770
It's a dense signal, but
it's not from the model.

01:24:35.770 --> 01:24:38.790
And on the other side, what we
just saw with preference tuning

01:24:38.790 --> 01:24:42.150
and with how to train
a reasoning model

01:24:42.150 --> 01:24:46.950
is that we're actually
leveraging outputs

01:24:46.950 --> 01:24:50.870
from the model at
training time, but we

01:24:50.870 --> 01:24:54.790
are relying on a sparse
signal, which is a reward that

01:24:54.790 --> 01:24:56.170
is at the sequence level.

01:24:56.170 --> 01:24:58.670
We don't have any
token-level way

01:24:58.670 --> 01:25:00.690
of just figuring
out what is good,

01:25:00.690 --> 01:25:04.910
what is not good,
at least for GRPO.

01:25:04.910 --> 01:25:10.320
And so there is a new set
of training strategies

01:25:10.320 --> 01:25:12.300
that are emerging.

01:25:12.300 --> 01:25:13.960
And there's one
such technique that

01:25:13.960 --> 01:25:16.300
has been becoming
more and more popular,

01:25:16.300 --> 01:25:20.920
which is called
on-policy distillation.

01:25:20.920 --> 01:25:22.480
So the idea behind
this method is

01:25:22.480 --> 01:25:26.880
to combine the best
of both worlds.

01:25:26.880 --> 01:25:33.760
So given a prompt, your model
is generating a response.

01:25:33.760 --> 01:25:38.360
But what you're doing
is instead of relying

01:25:38.360 --> 01:25:43.360
on a sparse signal that is
across the whole sequence,

01:25:43.360 --> 01:25:47.800
you're actually
asking a teacher model

01:25:47.800 --> 01:25:53.320
to evaluate every single
token that was generated.

01:25:53.320 --> 01:25:58.000
And you penalize deviations
between your model, the student

01:25:58.000 --> 01:26:00.280
model, and the teacher model.

01:26:00.280 --> 01:26:02.800
So at a very high
level, here, it's

01:26:02.800 --> 01:26:08.370
as if you were putting
the whole sequence

01:26:08.370 --> 01:26:09.670
that the student is generating.

01:26:09.670 --> 01:26:15.130
So think of it as just one at a
time, you generate the sequence.

01:26:15.130 --> 01:26:20.670
And you ask the teacher what
its prediction would be.

01:26:20.670 --> 01:26:24.410
And you compare the prediction
of the student and the teacher.

01:26:24.410 --> 01:26:27.930
And you penalize deviations.

01:26:27.930 --> 01:26:31.330
So the good thing here
is it is on-policy

01:26:31.330 --> 01:26:35.450
because you're actually using
the generation of the student.

01:26:35.450 --> 01:26:38.930
And it is also
dense because you're

01:26:38.930 --> 01:26:42.890
getting a token-level signal.

01:26:42.890 --> 01:26:45.785
So you have a few
variations of that strategy.

01:26:45.785 --> 01:26:47.410
So how do you come
up with the teacher?

01:26:47.410 --> 01:26:49.650
If a teacher is not there,
there is a variation

01:26:49.650 --> 01:26:53.490
called on-policy
self-distillation, which

01:26:53.490 --> 01:26:57.970
is you use the same
model for a task,

01:26:57.970 --> 01:27:01.930
but you tell that teacher
model, which is the same model,

01:27:01.930 --> 01:27:03.930
you tell it the answer.

01:27:03.930 --> 01:27:10.570
So you tell it the answer so it
knows how to steer its response.

01:27:10.570 --> 01:27:16.210
And this technique is being
incorporated in, I would say,

01:27:16.210 --> 01:27:19.930
most of the frontier
open white models.

01:27:19.930 --> 01:27:23.050
And as an example
here, you will see

01:27:23.050 --> 01:27:26.290
that you have setups
where you actually

01:27:26.290 --> 01:27:29.530
have specialized teachers--
for instance, one that

01:27:29.530 --> 01:27:32.770
is very good at math, one that
is very, very good at, let's

01:27:32.770 --> 01:27:34.010
say, engineering.

01:27:34.010 --> 01:27:38.490
And then what you do is you
perform a dense supervision

01:27:38.490 --> 01:27:42.970
of the model you're trying
to train versus these teacher

01:27:42.970 --> 01:27:46.010
models.

01:27:46.010 --> 01:27:50.490
And with that, I'll
give it to Shervine.

01:27:50.490 --> 01:27:52.170
Thank you, Afshine.

01:27:52.170 --> 01:27:54.330
So in this last
part, we're going

01:27:54.330 --> 01:27:57.270
to focus on a
practical use case.

01:27:57.270 --> 01:27:59.170
We're going to see
everything that Afshine

01:27:59.170 --> 01:28:01.970
mentioned in real life.

01:28:01.970 --> 01:28:08.340
So there is this website called
Artificial Analysis Intelligence

01:28:08.340 --> 01:28:10.060
Index that I recommend.

01:28:10.060 --> 01:28:11.940
In case you don't
know about it, it's

01:28:11.940 --> 01:28:14.460
an aggregator of
benchmarks, and it

01:28:14.460 --> 01:28:18.100
translates into a
single number, roughly

01:28:18.100 --> 01:28:20.000
what counts as
intelligence these days.

01:28:20.000 --> 01:28:23.900
So it's a good way to rank
models between each other.

01:28:23.900 --> 01:28:28.938
And there is this filter
called Open Source Models.

01:28:28.938 --> 01:28:30.480
So usually, these
open-source models,

01:28:30.480 --> 01:28:32.940
they are slightly
behind the true frontier

01:28:32.940 --> 01:28:35.000
from closed-source models.

01:28:35.000 --> 01:28:39.100
But the great thing about
them is that oftentimes they

01:28:39.100 --> 01:28:42.620
tell all the secret recipe
behind the preparation

01:28:42.620 --> 01:28:46.660
of these LLMs and
their open weights.

01:28:46.660 --> 01:28:49.120
And so we see this ranking.

01:28:49.120 --> 01:28:51.680
We have a few strong models.

01:28:51.680 --> 01:28:54.740
And there is one in
particular that has

01:28:54.740 --> 01:28:57.620
a great technical report, GLM--

01:28:57.620 --> 01:29:01.150
so GLM-5, which came
out earlier this year.

01:29:01.150 --> 01:29:04.230
And this will be one
that we will anchor on

01:29:04.230 --> 01:29:10.270
to see everything that Afshine
mentioned into practical terms.

01:29:10.270 --> 01:29:14.290
So we're going to go through
each stage of training

01:29:14.290 --> 01:29:16.050
the large language
model together,

01:29:16.050 --> 01:29:21.470
and then we're going to see what
they have put into each stage.

01:29:21.470 --> 01:29:23.630
So the first stage
that Afshine mentioned

01:29:23.630 --> 01:29:27.750
was pretraining, where the main
goal is to learn about language.

01:29:27.750 --> 01:29:31.110
So they put some web data.

01:29:31.110 --> 01:29:35.430
So what you use is
usually Common Crawl

01:29:35.430 --> 01:29:39.830
that has about everything
on the internet.

01:29:39.830 --> 01:29:42.870
And a lot of it these
days is polluted

01:29:42.870 --> 01:29:46.630
by AI-generated contents
and other things, which

01:29:46.630 --> 01:29:51.310
is why you have heuristics
based on this data that

01:29:51.310 --> 01:29:53.470
trims everything down.

01:29:53.470 --> 01:29:58.710
So you have some papers that
go into the reasoning of how

01:29:58.710 --> 01:29:59.820
you do that.

01:29:59.820 --> 01:30:03.560
Usually, you have about 1%
of your input in the end.

01:30:03.560 --> 01:30:05.400
So it's a great filter.

01:30:05.400 --> 01:30:08.720
And then it's a good
time to teach the model

01:30:08.720 --> 01:30:10.860
about main lines of knowledge.

01:30:10.860 --> 01:30:13.920
So usually, you have
authoritative websites,

01:30:13.920 --> 01:30:17.280
such as Wikipedia, that
give a lot of great things

01:30:17.280 --> 01:30:18.460
on general knowledge.

01:30:18.460 --> 01:30:20.880
But you have other
facts that might not

01:30:20.880 --> 01:30:25.080
be on these main websites, where
you need more care into curating

01:30:25.080 --> 01:30:28.200
what goes into your data mix.

01:30:28.200 --> 01:30:31.880
So Afshine mentioned
the reasoning aspect

01:30:31.880 --> 01:30:34.680
as being one that is very
important these days.

01:30:34.680 --> 01:30:38.440
So alongside coding, that is
at the foundation of everything

01:30:38.440 --> 01:30:41.760
that powers today's systems.

01:30:41.760 --> 01:30:44.280
So when we talk about
agents or we talk about them

01:30:44.280 --> 01:30:47.120
in the second part
of the class, these

01:30:47.120 --> 01:30:53.000
are mostly coding problems that
are put at different flavors, so

01:30:53.000 --> 01:30:55.560
which is why, as
early as pretraining,

01:30:55.560 --> 01:30:59.890
we want to train next-token
prediction on coding data-- so

01:30:59.890 --> 01:31:02.130
typically GitHub and the likes.

01:31:02.130 --> 01:31:04.130
And then sometimes you
have some languages

01:31:04.130 --> 01:31:07.830
that you care about that might
not be as present in your mix.

01:31:07.830 --> 01:31:13.050
So in particular,
mobile-specific data

01:31:13.050 --> 01:31:14.650
is an example of one.

01:31:14.650 --> 01:31:19.730
So this is where some
curation is needed.

01:31:19.730 --> 01:31:24.370
And then, finally,
you use material

01:31:24.370 --> 01:31:30.170
that has this
reasoning-like type of data

01:31:30.170 --> 01:31:32.310
to augment your training.

01:31:32.310 --> 01:31:36.210
So you also have things
like papers and math

01:31:36.210 --> 01:31:40.010
and science-focused.

01:31:40.010 --> 01:31:43.110
So in terms of numbers,
as Afshine mentioned,

01:31:43.110 --> 01:31:46.010
the pretraining stage
usually operates

01:31:46.010 --> 01:31:48.870
on a low-context-length regime.

01:31:48.870 --> 01:31:53.250
So GLM-5 was at
4K, about, tokens.

01:31:53.250 --> 01:31:55.570
And some orders of magnitude--

01:31:55.570 --> 01:31:59.490
so it's divided pretraining
into two stages, so first

01:31:59.490 --> 01:32:01.570
one that is very
coarse, where we just

01:32:01.570 --> 01:32:03.930
want to learn about
language structure,

01:32:03.930 --> 01:32:07.490
where we focus on web data.

01:32:07.490 --> 01:32:09.770
And then, once
this is done, then

01:32:09.770 --> 01:32:12.050
there is a mix of
coding and reasoning

01:32:12.050 --> 01:32:17.450
that comes in, alongside still
web, but at a lesser extent.

01:32:17.450 --> 01:32:20.130
And now I'm going to
talk about exactly

01:32:20.130 --> 01:32:22.070
what happens at training time.

01:32:22.070 --> 01:32:26.370
So there was a question here
on what do you actually learn.

01:32:26.370 --> 01:32:30.650
So here we learn purely
next-token prediction.

01:32:30.650 --> 01:32:34.230
And our goal is not to
generate useful sentences.

01:32:34.230 --> 01:32:38.890
We just want to learn large
concepts about language.

01:32:38.890 --> 01:32:42.330
So we do not have a
nicely-formatted speaking

01:32:42.330 --> 01:32:47.030
sequence end of sequence batch
that we just put it as is.

01:32:47.030 --> 01:32:51.210
We have a ton of
data that we chunk,

01:32:51.210 --> 01:32:52.890
and then we
concatenate documents

01:32:52.890 --> 01:32:56.200
that go together at once.

01:32:56.200 --> 01:33:00.540
So you will see things
like documents that

01:33:00.540 --> 01:33:04.680
are cut and then concatenated.

01:33:04.680 --> 01:33:06.980
And you don't necessarily
start your batch

01:33:06.980 --> 01:33:09.660
with the beginning-of-sequence
token, for example.

01:33:09.660 --> 01:33:16.860
So you do that in order to
fill your context length.

01:33:16.860 --> 01:33:19.300
And again, we mentioned a
typical order of magnitude

01:33:19.300 --> 01:33:21.420
is like a few K.

01:33:21.420 --> 01:33:26.140
And now I want to
focus a bit more

01:33:26.140 --> 01:33:30.300
on what I mentioned on
the importance of curating

01:33:30.300 --> 01:33:31.500
your data.

01:33:31.500 --> 01:33:38.080
So in the first LLMs that came
out in 2022, it was great.

01:33:38.080 --> 01:33:41.220
The internet was mostly
human-generated data.

01:33:41.220 --> 01:33:46.180
So you didn't need to worry
about the quality of who

01:33:46.180 --> 01:33:47.420
put data in.

01:33:47.420 --> 01:33:49.820
And I want to talk about
a phenomenon called

01:33:49.820 --> 01:33:52.070
model collapse.

01:33:52.070 --> 01:33:55.010
So who has heard
about model collapse?

01:33:55.010 --> 01:33:56.790
Yeah, a few folks.

01:33:56.790 --> 01:34:02.990
So the thing is, imagine
you have a perfect model,

01:34:02.990 --> 01:34:07.430
and let's say you have
human-generated data out there.

01:34:07.430 --> 01:34:09.310
Let's say you want
to train your model.

01:34:09.310 --> 01:34:13.750
You will sample a
finite number of samples

01:34:13.750 --> 01:34:16.830
from this larger corpus.

01:34:16.830 --> 01:34:18.950
But the thing is
the distribution

01:34:18.950 --> 01:34:22.710
that you have out
there is very diverse.

01:34:22.710 --> 01:34:25.990
So you might have some
nonzero probability for things

01:34:25.990 --> 01:34:28.470
that are super long-tail.

01:34:28.470 --> 01:34:31.490
And the thing is, since
your sampling is finite,

01:34:31.490 --> 01:34:35.350
you will not capture
all these details

01:34:35.350 --> 01:34:38.150
into training your
model, which means

01:34:38.150 --> 01:34:41.270
that the output of your
model is by nature doomed.

01:34:41.270 --> 01:34:44.310
So you will not have the
same distribution as output.

01:34:44.310 --> 01:34:47.890
And this is with the assumption
that the model is perfect.

01:34:47.890 --> 01:34:51.800
But on top of that, just imagine
the model is an approximator.

01:34:51.800 --> 01:34:54.140
It will not output the
perfect distribution.

01:34:54.140 --> 01:34:56.520
It is taught to learn.

01:34:56.520 --> 01:35:01.320
And to add insult to injury--
and you might have seen that

01:35:01.320 --> 01:35:03.840
into your own use of LLMs--

01:35:03.840 --> 01:35:07.000
you have, at later
stages, some preferences

01:35:07.000 --> 01:35:10.000
that vendors and
experts in language

01:35:10.000 --> 01:35:12.260
insert into the
behavior of the LLM.

01:35:12.260 --> 01:35:15.720
So if I talk to you about
things that are load-bearing,

01:35:15.720 --> 01:35:19.800
byte-identical, that might ring
a bell about your experience

01:35:19.800 --> 01:35:20.740
with LLMs.

01:35:20.740 --> 01:35:24.480
These are heavily upweighted
in frequency with respect

01:35:24.480 --> 01:35:26.160
to the true distribution.

01:35:26.160 --> 01:35:29.560
So this is another
problem that adds

01:35:29.560 --> 01:35:35.600
to this initial observation,
which is why all the curation

01:35:35.600 --> 01:35:39.480
techniques that these papers
try to achieve at first

01:35:39.480 --> 01:35:41.960
become really important.

01:35:41.960 --> 01:35:45.160
And then I know there was a
question a lecture or two ago

01:35:45.160 --> 01:35:49.790
about the number of epochs
that this kind of data runs on.

01:35:49.790 --> 01:35:53.010
So usually it's [? Of1. ?]
But in this paper,

01:35:53.010 --> 01:35:56.330
the data that is of higher
quality per classifiers

01:35:56.330 --> 01:36:00.130
might run for a bit more.

01:36:00.130 --> 01:36:03.590
So the second part,
which is midtraining--

01:36:03.590 --> 01:36:07.010
so Afshine mentioned it's where
you have higher-quality data

01:36:07.010 --> 01:36:08.350
come into the picture.

01:36:08.350 --> 01:36:11.610
And in the case
of GLM-5, the goal

01:36:11.610 --> 01:36:15.650
was purely to increase
context length,

01:36:15.650 --> 01:36:22.130
and do it in a way that works,
which is why the data mixture is

01:36:22.130 --> 01:36:26.370
mostly composed
longer-form data that

01:36:26.370 --> 01:36:28.870
matches the domains that
we're interested in.

01:36:28.870 --> 01:36:31.330
So for example, in
the case of code,

01:36:31.330 --> 01:36:35.190
we are looking at PRs with
issues and then code diffs.

01:36:35.190 --> 01:36:40.290
So these typically occupy
much more context length.

01:36:40.290 --> 01:36:47.010
And then you can afford to put
in entire books now and so on.

01:36:47.010 --> 01:36:50.130
And I had mentioned
last time the problem

01:36:50.130 --> 01:36:55.450
of context rot, where,
at larger context length,

01:36:55.450 --> 01:36:58.570
the LLM might have trouble
to remember a fact that

01:36:58.570 --> 01:37:00.570
was mentioned earlier on.

01:37:00.570 --> 01:37:03.170
So this is also a nice
place to take that

01:37:03.170 --> 01:37:07.930
into account, where,
in synthetic data,

01:37:07.930 --> 01:37:12.090
you might want to inject
some fact in the middle of it

01:37:12.090 --> 01:37:15.450
and then try to
refer of it later on.

01:37:15.450 --> 01:37:19.770
So it's another nice
place for doing so.

01:37:19.770 --> 01:37:22.850
And some orders
of magnitude-- so

01:37:22.850 --> 01:37:26.190
you have a similarly, [? then ?]
pretraining several stages--

01:37:26.190 --> 01:37:28.730
so a first one that
focuses on coding,

01:37:28.730 --> 01:37:34.730
and then a second one on
other longer-form data.

01:37:34.730 --> 01:37:38.150
And then the strategy to train
these is slightly different.

01:37:38.150 --> 01:37:41.150
So this paper mentions
exactly what's the difference.

01:37:41.150 --> 01:37:45.140
So here, instead of chunking
everything, just putting

01:37:45.140 --> 01:37:47.700
all to predict the
next token-- we

01:37:47.700 --> 01:37:49.700
are slightly smarter about it.

01:37:49.700 --> 01:37:53.460
We try to pack documents
in a way that they fit

01:37:53.460 --> 01:37:55.140
and that they're not cut.

01:37:55.140 --> 01:37:57.860
So it's called the
best-fit packing.

01:37:57.860 --> 01:38:05.820
And if there is too much
left, we may pad the context.

01:38:05.820 --> 01:38:07.740
And yeah, just like
pretraining, it's

01:38:07.740 --> 01:38:11.660
next token prediction, still.

01:38:11.660 --> 01:38:17.200
So now we want to make the
model a useful assistant.

01:38:17.200 --> 01:38:21.260
So this is the SFT
stage, where data

01:38:21.260 --> 01:38:23.800
that is of much higher
quality comes in.

01:38:23.800 --> 01:38:26.140
And then the orders of
magnitude of the tokens

01:38:26.140 --> 01:38:29.060
that go into training
just go down much more.

01:38:29.060 --> 01:38:32.180
So there is an emphasis
on general helpfulness.

01:38:32.180 --> 01:38:35.160
And then, just like Afshine
mentioned, today's models,

01:38:35.160 --> 01:38:38.480
they have seen that focusing
on reasoning is high-value.

01:38:38.480 --> 01:38:40.500
So there is a lot of
reasoning and encoding

01:38:40.500 --> 01:38:43.710
data injected there.

01:38:43.710 --> 01:38:47.950
And the paper
mentions that there

01:38:47.950 --> 01:38:52.630
is the strategy of inserting the
reasoning that leads to a given

01:38:52.630 --> 01:38:58.950
tool calls, and that is also
great for training the reasoning

01:38:58.950 --> 01:39:02.570
abilities when the model
wants to perform a tool call.

01:39:02.570 --> 01:39:07.010
So you will see interleaved
reasoning and the presence

01:39:07.010 --> 01:39:08.330
of think tokens.

01:39:08.330 --> 01:39:10.690
So I think we'll mention it
next time more in detail.

01:39:10.690 --> 01:39:13.470
So all this reasoning
that counts in your

01:39:13.470 --> 01:39:15.510
[? bill ?] and
that you don't see,

01:39:15.510 --> 01:39:19.110
the reason why you don't see
it is that it's between tokens

01:39:19.110 --> 01:39:20.990
called think tokens.

01:39:20.990 --> 01:39:23.230
So it's a way for
the model to have

01:39:23.230 --> 01:39:25.350
a scratchpad of
where it needs to go,

01:39:25.350 --> 01:39:29.030
but it doesn't
display it to you.

01:39:29.030 --> 01:39:32.990
And moving on, this
time, we do not

01:39:32.990 --> 01:39:35.370
do pure next-token prediction.

01:39:35.370 --> 01:39:37.790
So everything that's
about the input-- so let's

01:39:37.790 --> 01:39:40.700
say you are on a
general Q&amp;A use case.

01:39:40.700 --> 01:39:42.140
You have an input prompt.

01:39:42.140 --> 01:39:46.200
You do not try to guess
the next token there

01:39:46.200 --> 01:39:47.860
because it's a user input.

01:39:47.860 --> 01:39:51.680
What you want to fit a
next-token prediction on

01:39:51.680 --> 01:39:54.480
is the answer that you
want your LLM to have,

01:39:54.480 --> 01:39:59.200
which is why the loss
is more localized.

01:39:59.200 --> 01:40:02.600
And then the last stage,
RL-- so these days,

01:40:02.600 --> 01:40:07.040
RL is heavily used to develop
reasoning capabilities.

01:40:07.040 --> 01:40:11.320
And the GLM-5 paper divides
this into three parts.

01:40:11.320 --> 01:40:15.320
So the first one is on
reasoning RL, it calls.

01:40:15.320 --> 01:40:18.400
So for example, you have
these math competitions

01:40:18.400 --> 01:40:24.760
and other similar kinds of
data where you have a place

01:40:24.760 --> 01:40:26.880
to think about
what the answer is

01:40:26.880 --> 01:40:29.440
and an answer that
is verifiable.

01:40:29.440 --> 01:40:33.720
So this is the kind of
data that reasoning RL is.

01:40:33.720 --> 01:40:38.050
Agentic RL-- you put
your LLM into a harness,

01:40:38.050 --> 01:40:40.930
where it can use things
like running tests

01:40:40.930 --> 01:40:45.210
to verify that what it does
is correct-- for example,

01:40:45.210 --> 01:40:46.070
bug fixes.

01:40:46.070 --> 01:40:50.330
You might want to fix a
failing test to a passing test,

01:40:50.330 --> 01:40:52.410
and so on.

01:40:52.410 --> 01:40:56.090
And then general RL is
a bit more open-ended.

01:40:56.090 --> 01:41:00.630
So it comes up with dimensions
like foundational correctness,

01:41:00.630 --> 01:41:03.330
emotional intelligence,
and task-specific quality,

01:41:03.330 --> 01:41:07.770
which respectively denote
the quality of instruction

01:41:07.770 --> 01:41:08.610
following.

01:41:08.610 --> 01:41:13.690
Emotional intelligence is the
expert data curator's opinion

01:41:13.690 --> 01:41:17.610
about what a helpful answer
is, and so on, so they

01:41:17.610 --> 01:41:21.890
can inject that opinion there.

01:41:21.890 --> 01:41:26.890
And in this time-- so we'll
see more about it next time.

01:41:26.890 --> 01:41:28.950
So we generate the answer.

01:41:28.950 --> 01:41:32.250
And then, based on whether
the answer corresponds

01:41:32.250 --> 01:41:33.970
to what we're
looking for, we might

01:41:33.970 --> 01:41:37.820
want to upweight the probability
of some tokens happening

01:41:37.820 --> 01:41:41.100
versus not.

01:41:41.100 --> 01:41:45.220
And I want to connect
to the last topic

01:41:45.220 --> 01:41:46.680
that Afshine mentioned.

01:41:46.680 --> 01:41:51.540
So we have seen many stages
that try to do many things.

01:41:51.540 --> 01:41:54.260
And one thing you
might wonder about

01:41:54.260 --> 01:41:59.100
is, are we sure that
everything that we have learned

01:41:59.100 --> 01:42:00.820
is preserved?

01:42:00.820 --> 01:42:02.720
And it's a valid concern.

01:42:02.720 --> 01:42:09.940
It sometimes isn't, which is
why, at the last stage of RL,

01:42:09.940 --> 01:42:14.000
there is this stage of on-policy
cross-stage distillation,

01:42:14.000 --> 01:42:16.980
where the process that Afshine
mentioned is done against

01:42:16.980 --> 01:42:20.620
teachers that are at
previous checkpoints--

01:42:20.620 --> 01:42:23.900
so more specifically,
the one after SFT,

01:42:23.900 --> 01:42:28.780
and the one after
the first RL stage.

01:42:28.780 --> 01:42:36.460
So it's to make sure that we
haven't lost any capabilities.

01:42:36.460 --> 01:42:41.480
And so these open-weight
models, they're great.

01:42:41.480 --> 01:42:44.940
They give technical
papers with many details.

01:42:44.940 --> 01:42:48.060
And some of the secret
sauce behind the data

01:42:48.060 --> 01:42:50.220
isn't always spelled out.

01:42:50.220 --> 01:42:52.780
And I just want to say that--

01:42:52.780 --> 01:42:54.920
keep a critical eye
on everything you see,

01:42:54.920 --> 01:42:56.780
because you see
oftentimes, popping up

01:42:56.780 --> 01:43:03.020
in the news, some providers that
complain about others distilling

01:43:03.020 --> 01:43:03.760
their data.

01:43:03.760 --> 01:43:06.360
So this might be also
part of the data mixture,

01:43:06.360 --> 01:43:09.140
but of course not written
in the technical report.

01:43:09.140 --> 01:43:13.180
So there are some
allegations of it.

01:43:13.180 --> 01:43:19.220
And also, I want to mention the
case of Pi, the coding agent

01:43:19.220 --> 01:43:21.260
that you might know already.

01:43:21.260 --> 01:43:26.420
So this is a minimal agent
that boasts great performance

01:43:26.420 --> 01:43:28.660
with minimal harness.

01:43:28.660 --> 01:43:32.150
And the author, who I
believe was a precursor

01:43:32.150 --> 01:43:36.270
into understanding really well
how these models can perform

01:43:36.270 --> 01:43:41.590
great, he understood
that all these RL

01:43:41.590 --> 01:43:45.990
stages, they focus on developing
reasoning capabilities, which

01:43:45.990 --> 01:43:49.950
enable you to avoid
putting in too much context

01:43:49.950 --> 01:43:51.990
into your harness.

01:43:51.990 --> 01:43:54.130
So this is one
great consequence.

01:43:54.130 --> 01:43:58.230
And you might see the decrease
of the need of harness

01:43:58.230 --> 01:44:02.150
engineering more and
more thanks to that.

01:44:02.150 --> 01:44:05.770
And sharing things
recently in the news--

01:44:05.770 --> 01:44:09.950
so all this reasoning
data and training

01:44:09.950 --> 01:44:14.050
enables advances, such
as in the field of math.

01:44:14.050 --> 01:44:18.870
So very recently, OpenAI
released some solutions

01:44:18.870 --> 01:44:22.030
to open problems,
which is great.

01:44:22.030 --> 01:44:24.010
And with that, I hope
you have a great weekend.

01:44:24.010 --> 01:44:26.460
[APPLAUSE]

